// Recall-mode questions — General & Organic Chemistry
// Format: {s: tag, q: stem, choices: [4], ans: 0-3 index into choices, exp: explanation}
const QB_CHEMISTRY = [
  {s:'General Chemistry',q:'In element (nuclide) notation, A is the _____',choices:['atomic weight','atomic number','neutron number','mass number'],ans:3,exp:'A is the **mass number**: the total count of protons plus neutrons in the nucleus, always a whole number. Z is the atomic number (protons), the neutron number is A − Z, and the atomic weight is the decimal, abundance-weighted average shown on the periodic table.'},
  {s:'Atomic Structure',q:'In element (nuclide) notation, Z is the _____',choices:['neutron number','atomic weight','atomic number','mass number'],ans:2,exp:'Z is the **atomic number**, the number of protons, which defines the element. In a neutral atom it also equals the number of electrons; subtracting Z from the mass number A gives the number of neutrons.'},
  {s:'Atomic Structure',q:'_____ is the weighted average of the masses of an element\'s naturally occurring isotopes',choices:['Atomic number','Isotopic mass','Mass number','Atomic weight'],ans:3,exp:'**Atomic weight** averages the isotope masses weighted by natural abundance, which is why chlorine is listed as 35.45 rather than 35 or 37. Isotopic mass is the mass of one specific isotope, and the mass number is a whole-number count of nucleons.'},
  {s:'Atomic Structure',q:'The _____ states that an atom is mostly empty space, with electrons surrounding a small, dense, positively charged nucleus',choices:['Bohr model','Thomson model','Rutherford model','Dalton model'],ans:2,exp:'The **Rutherford model** came from the gold foil experiment: most alpha particles passed straight through, but a few bounced back off a tiny, dense, positive nucleus. It replaced Thomson\'s "plum pudding" model, in which positive charge was spread throughout the atom.'},
  {s:'Atomic Structure',q:'According to the _____, electrons orbit the nucleus in circular orbits that have a fixed size and quantized energy',choices:['Rutherford model','Thomson model','Dalton model','Bohr model'],ans:3,exp:'The **Bohr model** quantized electron orbits (E = −2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J × Z²/n²), which explains the line spectrum of hydrogen: a photon is absorbed or emitted only when an electron jumps between allowed levels. Rutherford\'s model had a nucleus but no fixed energy levels.'},
  {s:'General Chemistry',q:'Which principle states that the position and momentum of an electron cannot both be known exactly at the same time?',choices:['Pauli exclusion principle','Hund\'s rule','Aufbau principle','Heisenberg uncertainty principle'],ans:3,exp:'The **Heisenberg uncertainty principle** (Δx·Δp ≥ h/4π) means the more precisely position is known, the less precisely momentum can be known. This is why electrons are described by probability clouds (orbitals) rather than exact Bohr orbits.'},
  {s:'Atomic Structure',q:'_____ states that electrons fill each orbital of a subshell singly, with parallel spins, before any orbital in that subshell receives a second electron',choices:['Hund\'s rule','Pauli exclusion principle','Aufbau principle','Heisenberg uncertainty principle'],ans:0,exp:'**Hund\'s rule**: spreading electrons out with parallel spins minimizes electron–electron repulsion, so nitrogen\'s 2p³ has three unpaired electrons. The Pauli exclusion principle limits each orbital to two electrons of opposite spin, and the Aufbau principle sets the order in which subshells fill.'},
  {s:'Atomic Structure',q:'The _____ states that no two electrons in the same atom can have the same set of four quantum numbers',choices:['Hund\'s rule','Heisenberg uncertainty principle','Pauli exclusion principle','Aufbau principle'],ans:2,exp:'By the **Pauli exclusion principle**, two electrons sharing an orbital (same n, l, mₗ) must differ in spin (mₛ = +½ and −½), so each orbital holds at most two electrons. Hund\'s rule only governs how electrons spread across orbitals of equal energy.'},
  {s:'Constants',q:'Avogadro\'s number = _____',choices:['6.022 × 10²³ mol⁻¹','6.022 × 10⁻²³ mol⁻¹','6.626 × 10²³ mol⁻¹','1.381 × 10²³ mol⁻¹'],ans:0,exp:'**Avogadro\'s number**, 6.022 × 10²³, is the number of particles (atoms, molecules or ions) in one mole. Don\'t mix up its digits with Planck\'s constant (6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s), and remember the exponent is positive because a mole is a huge count.'},
  {s:'Constants',q:'Planck\'s constant, h, = _____',choices:['6.626 × 10³⁴ J·s','6.022 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s','6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s','6.626 × 10⁻²⁴ J·s'],ans:2,exp:'**Planck\'s constant**, h = 6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s, links a photon\'s energy to its frequency: E = hf = hc/λ. The tiny value is why quantum effects matter only at atomic scales; 6.022 belongs to Avogadro\'s number.'},
  {s:'Constants',q:'The speed of light in a vacuum, c, = _____',choices:['3.0 × 10⁶ m/s','3.0 × 10¹⁰ m/s','3.4 × 10² m/s','3.0 × 10⁸ m/s'],ans:3,exp:'The **speed of light** is c ≈ 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s, and c = fλ for all electromagnetic radiation. 3.0 × 10¹⁰ is the same speed in cm/s (a unit trap), and about 340 m/s is the speed of sound in air.'},
  {s:'Atomic Structure',q:'Diamagnetic materials are _____ an external magnetic field',choices:['attracted to','unaffected by','permanently magnetized by','repelled by'],ans:3,exp:'**Diamagnetic** substances have all electrons paired, so an applied field induces only a weak opposing magnetic moment and they are slightly repelled. Any unpaired electron makes a substance paramagnetic, which is attracted to the field instead.'},
  {s:'General Chemistry',q:'Paramagnetic materials are _____ an external magnetic field',choices:['repelled by','attracted to','unaffected by','permanently magnetized by'],ans:1,exp:'**Paramagnetic** substances have one or more unpaired electrons whose magnetic moments align with an applied field, so they are pulled into it (O₂ is the classic example). Unlike ferromagnets, they lose this alignment once the field is removed.'},
  {s:'General Chemistry',q:'Quantum number _____ is the principal quantum number and gives the electron\'s _____',choices:['n / energy level','l / energy level','n / orientation','mₛ / energy level'],ans:0,exp:'The **principal quantum number n** (1, 2, 3, …) gives the electron\'s shell: its energy level and average distance from the nucleus. A larger n means higher energy and a larger orbital; l, by contrast, sets the subshell shape.'},
  {s:'Atomic Structure: Quantum Numbers',q:'Quantum number _____ is the azimuthal quantum number and gives the _____ of an orbital',choices:['l / shape','n / shape','l / orientation','mₗ / shape'],ans:0,exp:'The **azimuthal quantum number l** (0 to n − 1) gives the subshell and orbital shape: l = 0 is s (spherical), 1 is p (dumbbell), 2 is d, 3 is f. Orientation in space is the job of mₗ, not l.'},
  {s:'Atomic Structure: Quantum Numbers',q:'Quantum number _____ is the magnetic quantum number and gives the orbital\'s _____',choices:['mₛ / orientation','mₗ / orientation','mₗ / shape','l / orientation'],ans:1,exp:'The **magnetic quantum number mₗ** (−l to +l) gives the orbital\'s orientation in space, e.g. px, py and pz for l = 1. The number of allowed mₗ values (2l + 1) equals the number of orbitals in the subshell.'},
  {s:'Atomic Structure: Quantum Numbers',q:'Quantum number _____ is the spin quantum number and gives the _____ of the electron',choices:['mₗ / spin','mₛ / orientation','mₛ / spin','n / spin'],ans:2,exp:'The **spin quantum number mₛ** is +½ or −½ and gives the electron\'s spin orientation. Two electrons in the same orbital must have opposite spins (Pauli exclusion); mₗ describes the orbital\'s orientation, not the electron\'s spin.'},
  {s:'Atomic Structure',q:'The maximum number of electrons in a single energy level (shell), in terms of the principal quantum number n, is _____',choices:['n²','2n','2n²','4n + 2'],ans:2,exp:'Shell n contains n² orbitals, each holding 2 electrons, so the maximum is **2n²** (n = 2 holds 8, n = 3 holds 18). The tempting n² counts orbitals, not electrons, and 4l + 2 is the formula for a subshell.'},
  {s:'Atomic Structure: Quantum Numbers',q:'The maximum number of electrons in a single subshell, in terms of the azimuthal quantum number l, is _____',choices:['2l + 1','4l','2l + 2','4l + 2'],ans:3,exp:'A subshell has 2l + 1 orbitals with 2 electrons each, giving **4l + 2** electrons: s holds 2, p holds 6, d holds 10, f holds 14. 2l + 1 is the number of orbitals, a common mix-up.'},
  {s:'Atomic Structure',q:'A/an _____ is an atom or molecule with an unpaired electron',choices:['free radical','isotope','ion','allotrope'],ans:0,exp:'A **free radical** has an unpaired valence electron, which makes it highly reactive (e.g., reactive oxygen species such as superoxide and the hydroxyl radical). An ion carries a net charge but may have all electrons paired, and an isotope differs only in neutron number.'},
  {s:'Atomic Structure: Quantum Numbers',q:'The azimuthal quantum number l = 0 designates which type of orbital?',choices:['p orbital','d orbital','f orbital','s orbital'],ans:3,exp:'l = 0 is the **s orbital**: spherical, one orientation, holding up to 2 electrons. The letters run s, p, d, f for l = 0, 1, 2, 3, holding 2, 6, 10 and 14 electrons respectively.'},
  {s:'Atomic Structure: Quantum Numbers',q:'The azimuthal quantum number l = 1 designates which type of orbital?',choices:['s orbital','p orbital','d orbital','f orbital'],ans:1,exp:'l = 1 is the **p orbital**: dumbbell-shaped, with three orientations (px, py, pz) holding up to 6 electrons. The p block spans Groups 13–18 of the periodic table.'},
  {s:'Atomic Structure: Quantum Numbers',q:'The azimuthal quantum number l = 2 designates which type of orbital?',choices:['s orbital','p orbital','d orbital','f orbital'],ans:2,exp:'l = 2 is the **d orbital**, with five orientations holding up to 10 electrons. The d block is the transition metals, and splitting of d-orbital energies in complexes explains their colors.'},
  {s:'Atomic Structure: Quantum Numbers',q:'The azimuthal quantum number l = 3 designates which type of orbital?',choices:['s orbital','p orbital','d orbital','f orbital'],ans:3,exp:'l = 3 is the **f orbital**, with seven orientations holding up to 14 electrons. The f block is the lanthanides and actinides; poor shielding by f electrons causes the lanthanide contraction.'},
  {s:'General Chemistry',q:'Which principle states that electrons fill lower-energy orbitals before moving to higher-energy orbitals?',choices:['Aufbau principle','Hund\'s rule','Heisenberg uncertainty principle','Pauli exclusion principle'],ans:0,exp:'The **Aufbau principle** ("building up") orders filling by energy, using the n + l rule: 1s, 2s, 2p, 3s, 3p, 4s, 3d, and so on, so 4s fills before 3d. Hund\'s rule governs how electrons spread within one subshell, not the order of subshells.'},
  {s:'Periodic Table',q:'Which group of elements has a single valence electron, forms +1 ions in compounds, and reacts vigorously with water?',choices:['Alkaline earth metals','Alkali metals','Halogens','Noble gases'],ans:1,exp:'**Alkali metals** (Group 1: Li, Na, K, Rb, Cs, Fr) readily lose their single ns¹ electron, giving them the lowest ionization energies in each period. They react with water to form H₂ gas and a metal hydroxide. Alkaline earth metals have two valence electrons and form +2 ions.'},
  {s:'Periodic Table',q:'Which group of elements forms +2 ions, reacts with water less vigorously than Group 1, and includes calcium and magnesium?',choices:['Alkali metals','Alkaline earth metals','Transition metals','Post-transition metals'],ans:1,exp:'**Alkaline earth metals** (Group 2: Be, Mg, Ca, Sr, Ba, Ra) have two ns² valence electrons and form +2 ions. Their higher effective nuclear charge makes them less reactive than the alkali metals; Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺ are key biological ions.'},
  {s:'Periodic Table',q:'Which class of elements is characterized by partially filled d orbitals, multiple oxidation states, and often forms colored compounds?',choices:['Alkali metals','Post-transition metals','Transition metals','Metalloids'],ans:2,exp:'**Transition metals** (Groups 3–12, the d block) can lose different numbers of s and d electrons, giving variable oxidation states (Fe²⁺/Fe³⁺, Cu⁺/Cu²⁺). Splitting of their d orbitals in complexes absorbs visible light, so their compounds are often colored.'},
  {s:'Periodic Table',q:'Which metals lie between the transition metals and the metalloids, include aluminum, tin and lead, and are softer with lower melting points than transition metals?',choices:['Alkali metals','Transition metals','Post-transition metals','Metalloids'],ans:2,exp:'**Post-transition metals** (e.g., Al, Ga, In, Sn, Tl, Pb, Bi) are p-block metals. They are softer and lower-melting than transition metals and more likely to form bonds with covalent character. Metalloids such as Si and Ge sit just to their right along the staircase.'},
  {s:'Periodic Table',q:'Which elements have properties intermediate between metals and nonmetals, are used as semiconductors, and include silicon and germanium?',choices:['Post-transition metals','Nonmetals','Noble gases','Metalloids'],ans:3,exp:'**Metalloids** (B, Si, Ge, As, Sb, Te) lie along the staircase line that separates metals from nonmetals. Their intermediate conductivity makes Si and Ge useful semiconductors.'},
  {s:'Periodic Table',q:'Which classification of elements includes hydrogen, carbon, nitrogen, and oxygen, and tends to gain electrons to form anions?',choices:['Metalloids','Nonmetals','Halogens','Alkali metals'],ans:1,exp:'**Nonmetals** (upper right of the table, plus hydrogen) have high ionization energies and electronegativities, so they tend to gain or share electrons, and they are poor conductors. The halogens are nonmetals too, but that group does not include H, C, N or O.'},
  {s:'Periodic Table',q:'Which group of elements has 7 valence electrons, most commonly forms −1 ions, and includes fluorine and chlorine?',choices:['Alkali metals','Noble gases','Alkaline earth metals','Halogens'],ans:3,exp:'**Halogens** (Group 17: F, Cl, Br, I, At) have an ns²np⁵ configuration and need one electron to complete the octet, so they form −1 halide ions. They are highly electronegative, and fluorine is the most electronegative element.'},
  {s:'Periodic Table',q:'Which group of elements has a full valence shell, is essentially chemically inert under normal conditions, and includes helium and argon?',choices:['Halogens','Alkali metals','Noble gases','Transition metals'],ans:2,exp:'**Noble gases** (Group 18: He, Ne, Ar, Kr, Xe, Rn) have filled valence shells (an octet, or a duet for He), giving them the highest ionization energies in their periods and almost no tendency to bond.'},
  {s:'Periodic Table',q:'Which class of elements occupies the two f-block rows at the bottom of the periodic table, the lanthanides and actinides?',choices:['Transition metals','Post-transition metals','Inner transition metals','Alkaline earth metals'],ans:2,exp:'The **inner transition metals** are the f block: the lanthanides (La–Lu) and actinides (Ac–Lr). Lanthanides behave very similarly because the added f electrons barely affect valence chemistry, and nearly all actinides are radioactive. "Rare earth" refers to the lanthanides plus Sc and Y, not the actinides.'},
  {s:'General Chemistry',q:'_____ is the net positive charge from the nucleus that is actually felt by valence electrons after shielding by inner electrons',choices:['Effective nuclear charge','Electron affinity','Ionization energy','Electronegativity'],ans:0,exp:'**Effective nuclear charge** (Zeff ≈ Z − number of core electrons) increases across a period, because protons are added while shielding stays nearly constant. That rising Zeff drives the periodic trends: smaller radius and higher ionization energy and electronegativity toward the upper right.'},
  {s:'Periodic Table: Periodic Trends',q:'_____ is the amount of energy required to remove an electron from a gaseous atom',choices:['Electron affinity','Atomic radius','Electronegativity','Ionization energy'],ans:3,exp:'**Ionization energy** is always endothermic (positive). It increases up and to the right as Zeff rises and radius shrinks, and it jumps sharply once a core electron must be removed (e.g., the second ionization energy of Na). Electron affinity is the energy change for adding an electron, not removing one.'},
  {s:'Periodic Table: Periodic Trends',q:'_____ is the amount of energy released when an electron is added to a gaseous atom',choices:['Electronegativity','Atomic radius','Electron affinity','Ionization energy'],ans:2,exp:'**Electron affinity** is greatest (most energy released) for the halogens, which need only one electron to fill their octet, and it generally increases up and to the right. Electronegativity is related but describes an atom\'s pull on shared electrons within a bond.'},
  {s:'Periodic Table: Periodic Trends',q:'_____ is a measure of how strongly an atom attracts the shared electrons in a chemical bond',choices:['Electron affinity','Electronegativity','Ionization energy','Atomic radius'],ans:1,exp:'**Electronegativity** (Pauling scale) increases up and to the right; F (4.0) is the most electronegative element, followed by O and N. The difference in electronegativity between two atoms (ΔEN) predicts whether a bond is nonpolar, polar or ionic.'},
  {s:'Bonding',q:'A/an _____ bond is the sharing of a pair of electrons between two atoms',choices:['ionic','hydrogen','covalent','metallic'],ans:2,exp:'In a **covalent** bond, two atoms (usually nonmetals) share an electron pair between their nuclei. Ionic bonds involve full electron transfer, and metallic bonding uses a delocalized "sea" of electrons rather than a localized shared pair.'},
  {s:'Bonding',q:'_____ bonds are those in which a single atom provides both bonding electrons',choices:['Polar covalent','Metallic','Ionic','Coordinate covalent'],ans:3,exp:'In a **coordinate covalent** (dative) bond, a Lewis base donates both electrons to a Lewis acid, as in NH₃ + H⁺ → NH₄⁺ or ligands bound to Fe²⁺ in heme. Once formed, the bond is indistinguishable from an ordinary covalent bond.'},
  {s:'Bonding',q:'_____ bonds are formed by the complete transfer of valence electron(s) between atoms',choices:['Covalent','Metallic','Ionic','Coordinate covalent'],ans:2,exp:'**Ionic** bonds form when ΔEN is large (> 1.7), typically between a metal and a nonmetal. The metal gives up electrons to become a cation, the nonmetal gains them to become an anion, and the ions are held together by electrostatic attraction (e.g., NaCl).'},
  {s:'Bonding',q:'_____ refers to the number of bonds between two atoms (single, double, or triple bond)',choices:['Bond polarity','Bond length','Bond order','Bond energy'],ans:2,exp:'**Bond order** counts the shared electron pairs between two atoms: 1 for single, 2 for double, 3 for triple. In MO theory it is ½(bonding e⁻ − antibonding e⁻). Bond energy and bond length both depend on bond order but are different quantities.'},
  {s:'Bonding',q:'The greater the bond order, the _____ the bond strength and the _____ the bond',choices:['higher / shorter','lower / longer','higher / longer','lower / shorter'],ans:0,exp:'More shared electron pairs pull the nuclei closer together, so a **higher** bond order gives a **stronger, shorter** bond: C≡C (~120 pm) < C=C (~134 pm) < C–C (~154 pm). A common trap is to assume a stronger bond must also be longer.'},
  {s:'Bonding',q:'Nonpolar covalent bonds have a ΔEN that is _____',choices:['greater than 1.7','less than 0.5','exactly 1.0','between 0.5 and 1.7'],ans:1,exp:'When ΔEN is **less than 0.5**, the electrons are shared about equally, so the bond is nonpolar (e.g., C–H, ΔEN ≈ 0.4; and any homonuclear bond such as H–H). ΔEN between 0.5 and 1.7 is polar covalent, and above 1.7 is generally ionic.'},
  {s:'Bonding',q:'Polar covalent bonds have a ΔEN that is between _____ and _____',choices:['0.5 / 1.7','0 / 0.5','1.7 / 3.0','1.0 / 2.5'],ans:0,exp:'Polar covalent bonds have ΔEN of about **0.5 to 1.7**: the electrons are shared unequally, giving partial charges (δ+/δ−), as in O–H (ΔEN ≈ 1.4). Below 0.5 the bond is essentially nonpolar; above 1.7 it is generally ionic.'},
  {s:'Bonding',q:'Cations are ions with a _____ charge',choices:['positive','negative','neutral','variable'],ans:0,exp:'**Cations** have lost electrons, so protons outnumber electrons and the charge is positive (e.g., Na⁺, Ca²⁺). Mnemonic: ca**t**ions are "pawsitive," and they migrate toward the cathode.'},
  {s:'Bonding',q:'Anions are ions with a _____ charge',choices:['positive','variable','negative','neutral'],ans:2,exp:'**Anions** have gained electrons, so electrons outnumber protons and the charge is negative (e.g., Cl⁻, O²⁻). Anions migrate toward the anode; nonmetals typically form anions and metals form cations.'},
  {s:'Bonding',q:'_____ are large, highly ordered, repeating three-dimensional arrays of ions',choices:['Amorphous solids','Metallic networks','Crystal lattices','Covalent network solids'],ans:2,exp:'Ionic compounds form **crystal lattices**, in which each cation is surrounded by anions (and vice versa) in a repeating pattern that maximizes attraction; this gives them high melting points. Amorphous solids lack long-range order, and covalent network solids (diamond, SiO₂) are built from atoms joined by covalent bonds, not ions.'},
  {s:'Intermolecular Forces',q:'Hydrogen bonds form when hydrogen is covalently bonded to which elements?',choices:['O, N and Cl','N, F and S','O, S and Cl','O, N and F'],ans:3,exp:'Hydrogen bonding requires H bonded to a small, highly electronegative atom (**F, O or N**, "H-FON"). This leaves H with a strong δ+ that is attracted to a lone pair on another F, O or N. Cl and S are too large and not electronegative enough to form true hydrogen bonds.'},
  {s:'Intermolecular Forces',q:'Van der Waals forces is a general term that includes _____ forces and _____ forces',choices:['London dispersion / dipole-dipole','covalent / ionic','ion-ion / metallic','covalent / London dispersion'],ans:0,exp:'**Van der Waals forces** are the weak intermolecular attractions between neutral molecules: London dispersion (induced dipoles) and dipole-dipole interactions. Covalent, ionic and metallic bonds are intramolecular or lattice bonds, not van der Waals forces.'},
  {s:'Intermolecular Forces',q:'_____ forces are attractive forces between the positive end of one polar molecule and the negative end of another polar molecule',choices:['Dipole-dipole','Hydrogen bonding','London dispersion','Ion-dipole'],ans:0,exp:'**Dipole-dipole** forces act between permanent dipoles, such as two acetone molecules, and raise the boiling point relative to nonpolar molecules of similar mass. Hydrogen bonding is an especially strong special case that requires H bonded to N, O or F.'},
  {s:'Intermolecular Forces',q:'_____ forces are temporary attractive forces created when a temporary dipole induces a dipole in a neighboring molecule',choices:['Dipole-dipole','London dispersion','Ion-dipole','Hydrogen bonding'],ans:1,exp:'**London dispersion** forces are present between all molecules and are the only intermolecular force between nonpolar ones. They grow with molecular size (polarizability) and surface area, which is why boiling point rises with chain length and branched isomers boil lower.'},
  {s:'General Chemistry',q:'_____ bonds are formed by head-on (end-to-end) overlap between atomic orbitals',choices:['Phi','Pi','Delta','Sigma'],ans:3,exp:'**Sigma (σ)** bonds concentrate electron density along the internuclear axis. Every single bond is a σ bond, and a double bond is one σ plus one π bond. Because the overlap is symmetric around the axis, groups can rotate freely about a σ bond.'},
  {s:'General Chemistry',q:'_____ bonds are formed by lateral (side-by-side) overlap of atomic orbitals',choices:['Phi','Sigma','Pi','Delta'],ans:2,exp:'**Pi (π)** bonds form from parallel unhybridized p orbitals, with electron density above and below the bond axis. They are weaker than σ bonds and prevent rotation, which is why cis/trans isomers exist about C=C double bonds.'},
  {s:'Bonding',q:'_____ is the charge assigned to an atom in a molecule assuming the electrons in every bond are shared equally',choices:['Partial charge','Oxidation number','Formal charge','Effective nuclear charge'],ans:2,exp:'**Formal charge** treats every bond as perfectly covalent: FC = valence e⁻ − nonbonding e⁻ − ½ bonding e⁻. The oxidation number makes the opposite assumption, giving all bonding electrons to the more electronegative atom, and a partial charge (δ) reflects the actual unequal sharing.'},
  {s:'Intermolecular Forces',q:'In a peptide (amide) bond, the carbonyl O acts as the hydrogen bond _____ and the N–H acts as the hydrogen bond _____',choices:['acceptor / donor','donor / acceptor','acceptor / acceptor','donor / donor'],ans:0,exp:'The carbonyl oxygen uses a lone pair to **accept** a hydrogen bond, and the N–H supplies the hydrogen as the **donor**. These C=O···H–N bonds between backbone groups stabilize α-helices and β-sheets (secondary structure).'},
  {s:'Bonding',q:'The bond angle of an sp hybridized atom is _____',choices:['90°','180°','109.5°','120°'],ans:1,exp:'An **sp** atom has two electron groups, which point in opposite directions to minimize repulsion: a linear geometry with a **180°** angle (e.g., CO₂, the carbons of an alkyne). The two leftover p orbitals form the π bonds.'},
  {s:'Bonding',q:'The bond angle of an sp² hybridized atom is _____',choices:['180°','120°','90°','109.5°'],ans:1,exp:'An **sp²** atom has three electron groups arranged trigonal planar at **120°** (e.g., BF₃, the carbons of an alkene or carbonyl). Its one unhybridized p orbital forms a π bond.'},
  {s:'Bonding',q:'The bond angle of an sp³ hybridized atom is _____',choices:['180°','109.5°','90°','120°'],ans:1,exp:'An **sp³** atom has four electron groups arranged tetrahedrally at **109.5°** (e.g., CH₄). Lone pairs compress the angle further: about 107° in NH₃ and 104.5° in H₂O.'},
  {s:'Bonding',q:'In an sp³d (trigonal bipyramidal) atom, the axial–equatorial bond angle is _____ and the equatorial–equatorial bond angle is _____',choices:['90° / 120°','120° / 90°','109.5° / 120°','90° / 180°'],ans:0,exp:'In an **sp³d** trigonal bipyramid (e.g., PCl₅), the three equatorial positions are **120°** apart in a plane, and each axial position is **90°** from them (the two axial positions are 180° apart). Lone pairs go equatorial first, where they have more room.'},
  {s:'Bonding',q:'The bond angle of an sp³d² hybridized atom is _____',choices:['109.5°','120°','90°','180°'],ans:2,exp:'An **sp³d²** atom has six electron groups arranged octahedrally, with every adjacent pair **90°** apart (e.g., SF₆); opposite (trans) positions are 180° apart. 109.5° belongs to the four-group tetrahedron.'},
  {s:'Bonding',q:'An H₂O molecule is bent because _____',choices:['the two lone pairs on oxygen repel the bonding pairs','the two hydrogen atoms attract each other strongly','oxygen is sp² hybridized with one empty p orbital','the polar O–H bonds pull the hydrogens together'],ans:0,exp:'Oxygen has four electron groups (two bonds, **two lone pairs**), so its electron geometry is tetrahedral, but only the atoms are "seen," giving a bent shape. Lone pairs repel more strongly than bonding pairs and squeeze the H–O–H angle to about 104.5°. The oxygen in water is sp³, not sp².'},
  {s:'Compounds',q:'The _____ formula gives the simplest whole-number ratio of atoms in a compound',choices:['molecular','structural','condensed','empirical'],ans:3,exp:'The **empirical** formula reduces subscripts to the lowest ratio: glucose, C₆H₁₂O₆, has the empirical formula CH₂O. The molecular formula is a whole-number multiple of it, found using the molar mass.'},
  {s:'General Chemistry',q:'The _____ formula shows the actual number of atoms of each element in one molecule',choices:['condensed','empirical','structural','molecular'],ans:3,exp:'The **molecular** formula gives the true atom counts (C₆H₁₂O₆ for glucose), while the empirical formula gives only the simplest ratio (CH₂O). Molecular formula = (molar mass ÷ empirical formula mass) × empirical formula.'},
  {s:'General Chemistry',q:'_____ is the exponent to which a reactant\'s concentration is raised in the rate law',choices:['Rate constant','Equilibrium constant','Activation energy','Reaction order'],ans:3,exp:'In rate = k[A]ᵐ[B]ⁿ, m and n are the **reaction orders** for A and B, and their sum is the overall order. They must be found by experiment, not from the balanced equation. The rate constant k is the proportionality factor and depends on temperature, not concentration.'},
  {s:'Chemical Kinetics',q:'A reaction whose rate does not depend on reactant concentration (rate = k) is _____',choices:['zeroth order','third order','second order','first order'],ans:0,exp:'When rate = k[A]⁰ = k, the reaction is **zeroth order**: the rate stays constant as [A] falls, and a plot of [A] vs. time is a straight line with slope −k. This happens when an enzyme or surface catalyst is saturated.'},
  {s:'Chemical Kinetics',q:'In _____ reactions, a single reactant breaks down into two or more products',choices:['decomposition','neutralization','combination','combustion'],ans:0,exp:'**Decomposition** (AB → A + B) is the reverse of a combination (synthesis) reaction, e.g. 2H₂O₂ → 2H₂O + O₂. Combustion requires O₂ as a second reactant.'},
  {s:'Chemical Kinetics',q:'_____ are exothermic reactions in which a fuel reacts with oxygen',choices:['Combustion reactions','Decomposition reactions','Synthesis reactions','Neutralization reactions'],ans:0,exp:'**Combustion** of a hydrocarbon or carbohydrate with O₂ produces CO₂ and H₂O and releases heat, e.g. CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O. Cellular respiration is a controlled, stepwise version of the combustion of glucose.'},
  {s:'Chemical Kinetics',q:'In a/an _____ reaction, an acid and a base react to form a salt and (usually) water',choices:['combustion','decomposition','displacement','neutralization'],ans:3,exp:'In **neutralization**, H⁺ from the acid and OH⁻ from the base combine to form water, and the remaining ions form a salt (HCl + NaOH → NaCl + H₂O). Strictly, it is a double-displacement reaction, but "neutralization" is the specific term for acid + base.'},
  {s:'General Chemistry',q:'A/an _____ reaction uses water to break the bonds in a molecule',choices:['combustion','condensation','hydrogenation','hydrolysis'],ans:3,exp:'**Hydrolysis** ("water-splitting") adds water across a bond to cleave it, as in digesting proteins, triglycerides and ATP. Condensation (dehydration synthesis) is the reverse: it joins two molecules and releases water.'},
  {s:'Chemical Kinetics',q:'If temperature goes up, the rate constant goes _____ and the reaction rate goes _____',choices:['up / up','down / up','up / down','down / down'],ans:0,exp:'By the Arrhenius equation, k = Ae^(−Ea/RT), raising T increases the fraction of collisions with energy ≥ Ea, so **k rises and so does the rate**. A rough rule: the rate about doubles for every 10 °C increase.'},
  {s:'Equilibrium',q:'The _____ is a measure of the relative amounts of products and reactants present in a reaction at any given moment, not necessarily at equilibrium',choices:['equilibrium constant','rate constant','solubility product','reaction quotient'],ans:3,exp:'The **reaction quotient Q** has the same form as Keq but uses the current concentrations. Comparing Q with Keq tells you which way the reaction will shift; Keq itself applies only at equilibrium.'},
  {s:'Equilibrium',q:'Pure solids and liquids are excluded from the reaction quotient and equilibrium constant because _____',choices:['they do not participate in the reaction','their activities are constant (equal to 1)','their concentrations cancel out mathematically','they are too dilute to affect equilibrium'],ans:1,exp:'The "concentration" of a pure solid or liquid is fixed by its density, so its **activity is 1** and it drops out of Q and K. The species still participates in the reaction; it just doesn\'t change the expression, as in Ksp = [Ag⁺][Cl⁻] for AgCl(s).'},
  {s:'Equilibrium',q:'If Q < Keq, the reaction will proceed _____',choices:['to the left, toward reactants','to the right, toward products','in neither direction','in a direction set only by temperature'],ans:1,exp:'When Q < Keq there are too few products relative to equilibrium, so the reaction shifts **right, toward products**, until Q rises to Keq. In this case ΔG = RT ln(Q/K) is negative, so the forward reaction is spontaneous.'},
  {s:'Equilibrium',q:'A reaction is at equilibrium if Q = _____',choices:['Keq','1','0','1/Keq'],ans:0,exp:'At equilibrium **Q = Keq**, so ΔG = RT ln(Q/K) = 0 and the forward and reverse rates are equal; there is no net change, though both reactions continue. Q = 1 means standard-state conditions, where ΔG = ΔG°.'},
  {s:'Equilibrium',q:'If Q > Keq, the reaction will proceed _____',choices:['in neither direction','to the left, toward reactants','in a direction set only by temperature','to the right, toward products'],ans:1,exp:'When Q > Keq there are too many products relative to equilibrium, so the reaction shifts **left, toward reactants**, until Q falls to Keq. ΔG is positive for the forward direction, so the reverse reaction is spontaneous.'},
  {s:'Chemical Kinetics',q:'Kinetic products are _____ in free energy compared to thermodynamic products and are favored at _____ temperatures',choices:['higher / lower','lower / higher','higher / higher','lower / lower'],ans:0,exp:'**Kinetic products** have a lower activation energy and form faster, but they sit **higher** in free energy (less stable). They dominate at **low** temperatures and short reaction times, when the reaction can\'t reverse; heating allows equilibration to the more stable thermodynamic product.'},
  {s:'General Chemistry',q:'Thermodynamic products are _____ in free energy compared to kinetic products, and they are _____ stable',choices:['higher / less','lower / less','lower / more','higher / more'],ans:2,exp:'**Thermodynamic products** are **lower** in free energy and therefore **more** stable, but they have a higher activation barrier. They are favored at high temperature and long reaction times, when the reaction is reversible and reaches equilibrium.'},
  {s:'Equilibrium',q:'Le Châtelier\'s principle states that if a stress is applied to a system at equilibrium, the system _____',choices:['shifts to maximize the reactants','stays at its original position','shifts to maximize the products','shifts to partially relieve the stress'],ans:3,exp:'**Le Châtelier\'s principle**: adding a reactant, removing a product, or changing pressure or temperature makes the system shift in the direction that counteracts the change. Only a temperature change alters Keq itself; concentration and pressure changes alter Q.'},
  {s:'Thermochemistry',q:'Standard conditions are _____ K, _____ atm, _____ M',choices:['273 / 1 / 1','310 / 1 / 1','298 / 1 / 1','298 / 760 / 1'],ans:2,exp:'**Standard state conditions** (used for ΔG°, ΔH° and E°) are 298 K (25 °C), 1 atm and 1 M. Don\'t confuse them with STP (273 K, 1 atm), which is used for gas-law calculations such as 22.4 L/mol; 760 is the pressure in torr, not atm.'},
  {s:'Thermochemistry',q:'_____ is the phase change from solid to liquid',choices:['Vaporization','Fusion','Sublimation','Freezing'],ans:1,exp:'**Fusion** (melting) is endothermic; the energy absorbed per mole is ΔH_fus. Freezing is the reverse, from liquid to solid, and releases the same amount of energy.'},
  {s:'Thermochemistry',q:'_____ is the phase change from liquid to solid',choices:['Vaporization','Fusion','Sublimation','Freezing'],ans:3,exp:'**Freezing** (solidification) is exothermic, because intermolecular attractions form as the particles lock into place. Fusion is the opposite process, from solid to liquid.'},
  {s:'Thermochemistry',q:'_____ is the phase change from liquid to gas',choices:['Fusion','Condensation','Vaporization','Freezing'],ans:2,exp:'**Vaporization** (boiling or evaporation) is endothermic; ΔH_vap is much larger than ΔH_fus because intermolecular attractions must be completely overcome. Condensation is the reverse, from gas to liquid.'},
  {s:'Thermochemistry',q:'_____ is the phase change from solid to gas',choices:['Vaporization','Fusion','Sublimation','Deposition'],ans:2,exp:'**Sublimation** skips the liquid phase, as with dry ice (CO₂) at 1 atm, and ΔH_sub = ΔH_fus + ΔH_vap. Deposition is the reverse, from gas directly to solid (e.g., frost).'},
  {s:'Thermochemistry',q:'The point on a phase diagram where all three phases coexist in equilibrium is known as the _____',choices:['boiling point','triple point','melting point','critical point'],ans:1,exp:'The **triple point** is the single temperature and pressure where the solid, liquid and gas lines meet. The critical point, by contrast, ends the liquid–gas line; beyond it the two phases can no longer be told apart.'},
  {s:'Thermochemistry',q:'Above the critical point, the densities of the liquid and gas become equal and the substance exists as a/an _____ fluid',choices:['supercooled','supercritical','superheated','subcritical'],ans:1,exp:'Past the critical temperature and pressure, the liquid–gas boundary disappears, giving a **supercritical fluid** with a density like a liquid\'s and a viscosity and diffusion like a gas\'s (e.g., supercritical CO₂ used to decaffeinate coffee). Superheated and supercooled describe metastable states below the critical point.'},
  {s:'Thermochemistry',q:'_____ combines enthalpy and entropy into a single value and is used to determine the spontaneity of a reaction',choices:['Entropy','Internal energy','Enthalpy','Gibbs free energy'],ans:3,exp:'**Gibbs free energy**: ΔG = ΔH − TΔS. A negative ΔG means the reaction is spontaneous at constant T and P. Neither ΔH nor ΔS alone can predict spontaneity, because each can be outweighed by the other term.'},
  {s:'General Chemistry',q:'If a reaction has a positive ΔH and a positive ΔS, then it will be _____ at _____ temperatures',choices:['spontaneous / high','spontaneous / low','non-spontaneous / all','spontaneous / all'],ans:0,exp:'In ΔG = ΔH − TΔS, a positive ΔH opposes the reaction but the −TΔS term favors it. ΔG turns negative only when TΔS outweighs ΔH, so the reaction is **spontaneous at high temperatures** (above T = ΔH/ΔS), as with ice melting above 0 °C.'},
  {s:'Thermochemistry',q:'If a reaction has a positive ΔH and a negative ΔS, then it will be _____ at _____ temperatures',choices:['spontaneous / high','spontaneous / low','spontaneous / all','non-spontaneous / all'],ans:3,exp:'With ΔH > 0 and ΔS < 0, both ΔH and −TΔS are positive, so ΔG > 0 at every temperature: **non-spontaneous at all temperatures**. The reverse reaction is spontaneous at all temperatures.'},
  {s:'Thermochemistry',q:'If a reaction has a negative ΔH and a positive ΔS, then it will be _____ at _____ temperatures',choices:['spontaneous / all','spontaneous / high','spontaneous / low','non-spontaneous / all'],ans:0,exp:'With ΔH < 0 and ΔS > 0, both terms of ΔG = ΔH − TΔS are negative, so ΔG < 0 at every temperature: the reaction is **always spontaneous** (e.g., many combustions).'},
  {s:'Thermochemistry',q:'If a reaction has a negative ΔH and a negative ΔS, then it will be _____ at _____ temperatures',choices:['spontaneous / high','spontaneous / low','spontaneous / all','non-spontaneous / all'],ans:1,exp:'With ΔH < 0 and ΔS < 0, ΔG is negative only while |ΔH| > T|ΔS|, i.e. the reaction is **spontaneous at low temperatures**. Above T = ΔH/ΔS it becomes non-spontaneous (e.g., water freezing only below 0 °C).'},
  {s:'Thermochemistry',q:'Room temperature (the standard-state temperature) is approximately _____',choices:['25 °C','0 °C','37 °C','100 °C'],ans:0,exp:'Standard-state "room temperature" is **25 °C (298 K)**. 0 °C (273 K) is the STP temperature, 37 °C is body temperature, and 100 °C is the boiling point of water at 1 atm.'},
  {s:'Thermochemistry',q:'Normal human body temperature is approximately _____',choices:['37 °C','25 °C','42 °C','30 °C'],ans:0,exp:'Normal core body temperature is about **37 °C (310 K, 98.6 °F)**; physiology passages often use 310 K in calculations with the Nernst equation or with ΔG. 25 °C (298 K) is the standard-state temperature.'},
  {s:'General Chemistry',q:'_____ is the heat content of a system at constant pressure, reflecting the potential energy stored in chemical bonds and intermolecular attractions',choices:['Enthalpy (H)','Internal energy (U)','Entropy (S)','Gibbs free energy (G)'],ans:0,exp:'**Enthalpy** (H = U + PV) changes as bonds and attractions are broken (endothermic, +ΔH) or formed (exothermic, −ΔH). ΔH equals the heat exchanged at constant pressure (q_p). Entropy measures energy dispersal, not stored energy.'},
  {s:'Thermochemistry',q:'Phase changes from solid → liquid → gas are _____',choices:['adiabatic','isothermal','endothermic','exothermic'],ans:2,exp:'Melting and vaporization absorb heat to overcome intermolecular attractions, so they are **endothermic** (ΔH > 0) and also increase entropy (ΔS > 0). Temperature stays constant during the phase change itself because the absorbed heat goes into breaking attractions, not into kinetic energy.'},
  {s:'Thermochemistry',q:'Phase changes from gas → liquid → solid are _____',choices:['isothermal','adiabatic','exothermic','endothermic'],ans:2,exp:'Condensation and freezing release heat as intermolecular attractions form, so they are **exothermic** (ΔH < 0) and decrease entropy (ΔS < 0). This is why steam burns are worse than burns from boiling water: condensing steam releases ΔH_vap onto the skin.'},
  {s:'Thermochemistry',q:'_____ is a measure of how much energy has spread out, or how spread out energy has become, in a system',choices:['Enthalpy','Internal energy','Entropy','Gibbs free energy'],ans:2,exp:'**Entropy (S)** measures energy dispersal, or the number of available microstates. It increases from solid to liquid to gas, on dissolving, and when the moles of gas increase. ΔS_universe > 0 for every spontaneous process (second law).'},
  {s:'Thermochemistry',q:'If ΔG < 0, the reaction will be _____',choices:['spontaneous','non-spontaneous','at equilibrium','endothermic'],ans:0,exp:'A negative ΔG means the forward reaction is **spontaneous** (exergonic) and Keq > 1 (ΔG° = −RT ln Keq). Spontaneous says nothing about speed: a reaction with ΔG < 0 can still be very slow if Ea is high.'},
  {s:'Thermochemistry',q:'If ΔG = 0, the reaction will be _____',choices:['spontaneous','endothermic','non-spontaneous','at equilibrium'],ans:3,exp:'ΔG = 0 means the system is **at equilibrium**: the forward and reverse rates are equal and Q = Keq. Phase changes at their transition temperature (e.g., ice and water at 0 °C) are a classic example.'},
  {s:'Thermochemistry',q:'If ΔG > 0, the reaction will be _____',choices:['at equilibrium','exothermic','non-spontaneous','spontaneous'],ans:2,exp:'A positive ΔG means the forward reaction is **non-spontaneous** (endergonic) and the reverse reaction is spontaneous. In cells, such reactions are driven by coupling them to ATP hydrolysis, so that the overall ΔG is negative.'},
  {s:'Atomic Structure',q:'Electron orbits that are farther from the nucleus have _____ energy',choices:['the same','higher','zero','lower'],ans:1,exp:'In the Bohr model, E = −R_H/n²: as n increases the energy becomes less negative, i.e. **higher**, and it approaches 0 at ionization. Electrons farther out are less tightly bound, which is why valence electrons are the ones removed first.'},
  {s:'Atomic Structure',q:'If an electron moves to an orbit farther from the nucleus, a photon is _____',choices:['scattered','absorbed','reflected','emitted'],ans:1,exp:'Moving to a higher energy level requires energy, so a photon is **absorbed**, with energy exactly equal to the gap (ΔE = hf). This produces an absorption spectrum; dropping back down emits a photon of the same energy.'},
  {s:'Atomic Structure',q:'If an electron moves to an orbit closer to the nucleus, a photon is _____',choices:['scattered','reflected','absorbed','emitted'],ans:3,exp:'Dropping to a lower energy level releases the energy difference as a photon, so a photon is **emitted** (ΔE = hf = hc/λ). This produces emission line spectra such as hydrogen\'s Balmer series, which comes from transitions down to n = 2.'},
  {s:'Bonding',q:'Ionic bonds have a ΔEN that is _____',choices:['exactly 0','between 0.5 and 1.7','greater than 1.7','less than 0.5'],ans:2,exp:'When ΔEN is **greater than 1.7**, the more electronegative atom effectively takes the electron(s), forming ions, as in NaCl (ΔEN ≈ 2.1). Between 0.5 and 1.7 the electrons are only unequally shared (polar covalent).'},
  {s:'Bonding',q:'The orbital hybridization of an atom with 2 electron groups is _____',choices:['sp','sp³','sp³d','sp²'],ans:0,exp:'Two electron groups need two hybrid orbitals, made from one s and one p orbital: **sp**, linear at 180°. Count the electron groups (bonds of any order count once, plus lone pairs) to get the hybridization: 2 = sp, 3 = sp², 4 = sp³.'},
  {s:'Bonding',q:'The electronic geometry of an atom with 2 electron groups is _____',choices:['linear','bent','tetrahedral','trigonal planar'],ans:0,exp:'Two electron groups get as far apart as possible at 180°, giving a **linear** geometry (e.g., CO₂, BeCl₂). Bent is a molecular shape that arises from three or four electron groups with lone pairs, not from two groups.'},
  {s:'Bonding',q:'The orbital hybridization of an atom with 3 electron groups is _____',choices:['sp','sp²','sp³','sp³d'],ans:1,exp:'Three electron groups use one s and two p orbitals: **sp²**, trigonal planar at 120°. The one leftover p orbital can form a π bond, which is why alkene and carbonyl carbons are sp².'},
  {s:'Bonding',q:'The electronic geometry of an atom with 3 electron groups is _____',choices:['tetrahedral','trigonal bipyramidal','linear','trigonal planar'],ans:3,exp:'Three electron groups spread out at 120° in one plane: **trigonal planar** (e.g., BF₃, the carbonyl carbon). With one lone pair, the molecular shape becomes bent (e.g., SO₂), but the electronic geometry is still trigonal planar.'},
  {s:'Bonding',q:'The orbital hybridization of an atom with 4 electron groups is _____',choices:['sp²','sp³d','sp³','sp³d²'],ans:2,exp:'Four electron groups use one s and three p orbitals: **sp³**, tetrahedral at 109.5°. This holds even when some groups are lone pairs, so the central atoms of NH₃ and H₂O are also sp³.'},
  {s:'Bonding',q:'The electronic geometry of an atom with 4 electron groups is _____',choices:['octahedral','trigonal planar','tetrahedral','linear'],ans:2,exp:'Four electron groups point to the corners of a **tetrahedron** (109.5°). The molecular shape depends on how many are lone pairs: tetrahedral (CH₄), trigonal pyramidal (NH₃) or bent (H₂O).'},
  {s:'Bonding',q:'The orbital hybridization of an atom with 5 electron groups is _____',choices:['sp³','sp³d','sp³d²','sp²'],ans:1,exp:'Five electron groups require one s, three p and one d orbital: **sp³d**, trigonal bipyramidal (e.g., PCl₅). Only atoms in period 3 or below can expand their octet this way, because they have d orbitals available.'},
  {s:'Bonding',q:'The electronic geometry of an atom with 5 electron groups is _____',choices:['linear','tetrahedral','trigonal bipyramidal','octahedral'],ans:2,exp:'Five electron groups form a **trigonal bipyramid**: three equatorial positions at 120° and two axial positions at 90° to them. Lone pairs occupy equatorial sites, giving seesaw (SF₄), T-shaped (ClF₃) or linear (XeF₂) molecular shapes.'},
  {s:'Bonding',q:'The orbital hybridization of an atom with 6 electron groups is _____',choices:['sp³d','sp³','sp³d²','sp²'],ans:2,exp:'Six electron groups need six hybrid orbitals: one s + three p + two d = **sp³d²**, giving octahedral electron geometry (e.g., SF₆). Count orbitals to check: sp³d has only five, for trigonal bipyramidal atoms.'},
  {s:'Bonding',q:'The electronic geometry of an atom with 6 electron groups is _____',choices:['trigonal bipyramidal','octahedral','square planar','tetrahedral'],ans:1,exp:'Six electron groups spread out to the six vertices of an **octahedron** with 90° angles (sp³d²). Square planar is a molecular geometry (6 groups with 2 lone pairs, e.g., XeF₄), not an electronic geometry.'},
  {s:'Equilibrium',q:'_____ is the value of the reaction quotient at chemical equilibrium',choices:['The rate constant (k)','The equilibrium constant (Keq)','Henry\'s law constant (kH)','The reaction quotient (Q)'],ans:1,exp:'Q can take any value as a reaction proceeds; when the system reaches equilibrium, Q equals the **equilibrium constant (Keq)**. Keq depends only on temperature, while the rate constant k describes speed, not position, of equilibrium.'},
  {s:'Atomic Structure',q:'Protons and electrons have _____ masses',choices:['identical','approximately equal','very different','equal but opposite'],ans:2,exp:'A proton is about 1836 times heavier than an electron (≈1 amu vs ≈0.0005 amu), so their masses are **very different**. It is their charges, not their masses, that are equal in magnitude but opposite in sign.'},
  {s:'Solutions',q:'A _____ is a homogeneous mixture of two or more substances in a single phase',choices:['solution','colloid','suspension','emulsion'],ans:0,exp:'A **solution** is uniform at the molecular level: solute particles are dispersed individually and never settle out. Colloids, suspensions and emulsions contain larger dispersed particles and are not truly homogeneous.'},
  {s:'Solutions',q:'The _____ is the component (usually present in the greater amount) that dissolves a solid, liquid or gaseous _____',choices:['solvent / solute','solute / solvent','solvent / solution','solute / solution'],ans:0,exp:'The **solvent** does the dissolving and is usually the major component; the **solute** is the substance dissolved, and it can be a solid, liquid or gas. Together they make up the solution.'},
  {s:'Solutions',q:'_____ or _____ is the process of dissolving a solute in a solvent',choices:['Solvation / dissolution','Solvation / crystallization','Precipitation / crystallization','Sublimation / deposition'],ans:0,exp:'In **solvation (dissolution)** solvent molecules surround and interact with solute particles; when water is the solvent it is called hydration. Precipitation and crystallization are the reverse, with solute leaving solution.'},
  {s:'Solutions',q:'_____ is measured in terms of the maximum amount of solute dissolved in a solvent at equilibrium',choices:['Concentration','Molarity','Solubility','Solubility product'],ans:2,exp:'**Solubility** is the maximum amount of solute that dissolves at a given temperature, giving a saturated solution at equilibrium. Concentration and molarity describe any solution, saturated or not, and Ksp is an equilibrium constant rather than an amount.'},
  {s:'Solutions',q:'_____ is the number of moles of a substance that can be dissolved per liter of solution before the solution becomes saturated',choices:['Solubility product','Normality','Molar solubility','Molarity'],ans:2,exp:'**Molar solubility** is solubility expressed in mol/L of a saturated solution. It can be calculated from Ksp (e.g., for AB, Ksp = x², so x = √Ksp), while molarity applies to any solution, saturated or not.'},
  {s:'Solutions',q:'A/an _____ has a metal ion at its center with a number of other molecules or ions surrounding it',choices:['chelate','ligand','complex ion','polyatomic ion'],ans:2,exp:'A **complex ion** forms when Lewis-base ligands donate electron pairs to a central metal cation (a Lewis acid) through coordinate covalent bonds, e.g., [Cu(NH₃)₄]²⁺. The surrounding molecules are the ligands; a chelate is the special case in which one ligand binds at several sites.'},
  {s:'Solutions',q:'Polar molecules are _____, while nonpolar molecules are _____',choices:['hydrophilic / hydrophobic','hydrophobic / hydrophilic','hydrophilic / amphipathic','amphipathic / hydrophilic'],ans:0,exp:'Like dissolves like: polar molecules interact favorably with water (**hydrophilic**), while nonpolar molecules cannot hydrogen bond with water and are excluded from it (**hydrophobic**). Amphipathic molecules such as phospholipids have both regions.'},
  {s:'Solutions',q:'For a 1 M acid solution, the normality equals the _____',choices:['number of H⁺ ions each acid molecule can donate','number of moles of acid per kilogram of solvent','mass of acid per liter of solution','number of particles each formula unit dissolves into'],ans:0,exp:'Normality = molarity × number of equivalents per molecule; for an acid that is the number of H⁺ it can donate, so 1 M H₂SO₄ is 2 N. Moles per kilogram of solvent is molality, and particles per formula unit is the van \'t Hoff factor.'},
  {s:'Solutions: Solubility',q:'Salts containing Na⁺, K⁺, NH₄⁺ or NO₃⁻ are _____ soluble',choices:['never','sometimes','only in acidic solution','always'],ans:3,exp:'Alkali metal, ammonium and nitrate salts are **always** soluble in water; this is the first and most reliable solubility rule. Acetate (CH₃COO⁻) salts are also nearly always soluble.'},
  {s:'Solutions: Solubility',q:'Cl⁻, Br⁻ and I⁻ salts are _____ except with Pb²⁺, Hg₂²⁺ and Ag⁺',choices:['slightly soluble','insoluble','soluble','always soluble'],ans:2,exp:'Halide salts are generally **soluble**; the exceptions are the lead, mercury(I) and silver halides (e.g., AgCl precipitates). Mnemonic for the exceptions: "PHAge" (Pb, Hg₂, Ag).'},
  {s:'Solutions: Solubility',q:'SO₄²⁻ salts are _____ except with Ca²⁺, Sr²⁺, Ba²⁺, Pb²⁺, Hg₂²⁺ and Ag⁺',choices:['insoluble','slightly soluble','soluble','always soluble'],ans:2,exp:'Sulfates are generally **soluble**, but the heavier alkaline earth, lead, mercury(I) and silver sulfates are insoluble or only slightly soluble. BaSO₄ is so insoluble that it is used as an oral contrast agent in radiology.'},
  {s:'Solutions: Solubility',q:'S²⁻ salts are _____ except with Na⁺, K⁺, NH₄⁺, Mg²⁺, Ca²⁺, Sr²⁺ and Ba²⁺',choices:['always soluble','insoluble','soluble','slightly soluble'],ans:1,exp:'Sulfides are generally **insoluble** (e.g., the black precipitates PbS, CuS, Ag₂S); only the alkali metal, ammonium and alkaline earth sulfides dissolve.'},
  {s:'Solutions: Solubility',q:'O²⁻ salts (oxides) are _____ except with Na⁺, K⁺, Sr²⁺ and Ba²⁺',choices:['soluble','always soluble','insoluble','slightly soluble'],ans:2,exp:'Most metal oxides are **insoluble**. The alkali metal oxides and the heavier alkaline earth oxides dissolve by reacting with water to form the corresponding soluble hydroxides (e.g., Na₂O + H₂O → 2NaOH).'},
  {s:'Solutions: Solubility',q:'OH⁻ salts are _____ except with Na⁺, K⁺, Ca²⁺, Sr²⁺ and Ba²⁺',choices:['always soluble','slightly soluble','soluble','insoluble'],ans:3,exp:'Hydroxides are generally **insoluble** (e.g., Fe(OH)₃, Cu(OH)₂, Mg(OH)₂). The alkali metal hydroxides are soluble strong bases, and Ca, Sr and Ba hydroxides are soluble enough to count as exceptions (Ca(OH)₂ only slightly).'},
  {s:'Solutions: Solubility',q:'CrO₄²⁻ (chromate) salts are _____ except with Na⁺, K⁺, NH₄⁺ and Mg²⁺',choices:['always soluble','slightly soluble','insoluble','soluble'],ans:2,exp:'Chromate salts are generally **insoluble** (e.g., Ag₂CrO₄, PbCrO₄, BaCrO₄), except with the alkali metals, ammonium and Mg²⁺. Note the formula: chromate is CrO₄²⁻, while dichromate is Cr₂O₇²⁻.'},
  {s:'Solutions: Solubility',q:'PO₄³⁻ and CO₃²⁻ salts are _____ except with Na⁺, K⁺ and NH₄⁺',choices:['slightly soluble','insoluble','always soluble','soluble'],ans:1,exp:'Phosphates and carbonates are generally **insoluble** (e.g., CaCO₃ in limestone, Ca₃(PO₄)₂ in bone); only the alkali metal and ammonium salts dissolve. They do dissolve in acid, because the anions are protonated.'},
  {s:'Solutions: Solubility',q:'Ksp is the _____ for a reaction that consists of a solid dissolving in a solution',choices:['equilibrium constant','Henry\'s law constant','rate constant','reaction quotient'],ans:0,exp:'The solubility product **Ksp** is the equilibrium constant for a solid salt dissolving into its ions, e.g., AgCl(s) ⇌ Ag⁺ + Cl⁻; it is fixed at a given temperature. The reaction quotient Qsp has the same form but uses the current, not equilibrium, ion concentrations.'},
  {s:'Solutions: Solubility',q:'For BaSO₄(s) ⇌ Ba²⁺(aq) + SO₄²⁻(aq), Ksp = _____',choices:['[Ba²⁺][SO₄²⁻]','[Ba²⁺][SO₄²⁻] / [BaSO₄]','[BaSO₄] / ([Ba²⁺][SO₄²⁻])','[Ba²⁺]²[SO₄²⁻]'],ans:0,exp:'Ksp is the product of the dissolved ion concentrations, each raised to its stoichiometric coefficient (here both 1). The solid BaSO₄ is left out because a pure solid has an activity of 1.'},
  {s:'Solutions: Solubility',q:'Qsp is the _____ for a reaction that consists of a solid dissolving in a solution',choices:['equilibrium constant','activity','reaction quotient','rate constant'],ans:2,exp:'**Qsp** is the ion product calculated from the current ion concentrations, whether or not the solution is at equilibrium. Comparing Qsp with Ksp tells you whether a precipitate will form.'},
  {s:'Solutions: Solubility',q:'If Qsp is _____ Ksp, the solution is unsaturated, so no precipitate will form',choices:['equal to','greater than','less than','unrelated to'],ans:2,exp:'When Qsp < Ksp, the ion concentrations are below their equilibrium values, so more solid can still dissolve and nothing precipitates. Qsp = Ksp is saturated; Qsp > Ksp is supersaturated and precipitates.'},
  {s:'Solutions: Solubility',q:'If Qsp is _____ Ksp, the solution is saturated and at equilibrium, so no precipitate will form',choices:['greater than','unrelated to','equal to','less than'],ans:2,exp:'When Qsp = Ksp, the solution holds the maximum amount of dissolved ions and dissolution and precipitation occur at equal rates. Adding more ions would make Qsp > Ksp and cause precipitation.'},
  {s:'Solutions: Solubility',q:'If Qsp is _____ Ksp, the solution is supersaturated, so a precipitate will form',choices:['unrelated to','greater than','less than','equal to'],ans:1,exp:'When Qsp > Ksp, the ion concentrations exceed what equilibrium allows, so the reaction shifts left and solid precipitates until Qsp falls back to Ksp. This is the same logic as Q > K shifting any reaction toward reactants.'},
  {s:'Solutions: Solubility',q:'The common ion effect causes solubility to _____ when like ions are added',choices:['double','remain unchanged','decrease','increase'],ans:2,exp:'Adding an ion already in the equilibrium (e.g., Cl⁻ to a saturated AgCl solution) raises Qsp above Ksp, so by Le Châtelier\'s principle the equilibrium shifts toward the solid and solubility **decreases**. Ksp itself does not change, only the molar solubility.'},
  {s:'Solutions',q:'A/an _____ is a compound with a central cation bonded to the same ligand in multiple places',choices:['chelate','monodentate complex','ligand','coordination isomer'],ans:0,exp:'A **chelate** forms when a multidentate (polydentate) ligand such as EDTA binds one metal ion at two or more sites, forming a stable ring. Chelation therapy (EDTA for lead poisoning) and heme binding iron are biological examples.'},
  {s:'Solutions',q:'_____ are physical properties of solutions that depend on the concentration of dissolved particles but not on their chemical identity',choices:['Thermodynamic properties','Extensive properties','Intensive properties','Colligative properties'],ans:3,exp:'**Colligative properties** depend only on the number of solute particles: vapor pressure lowering, boiling point elevation, freezing point depression and osmotic pressure. That is why the van \'t Hoff factor i (particles per formula unit) appears in their equations.'},
  {s:'Solutions',q:'Raoult\'s law states that the vapor pressure of a solution equals _____',choices:['the sum of each volatile component\'s mole fraction times its pure vapor pressure','the pure solvent\'s vapor pressure times the solute\'s molality','the product of the pure vapor pressures of all the components','the atmospheric pressure minus the osmotic pressure of the solution'],ans:0,exp:'Raoult\'s law: P_solution = ΣXᵢPᵢ°. Adding a nonvolatile solute lowers the solvent\'s mole fraction and therefore the vapor pressure, which underlies the other colligative properties.'},
  {s:'Solutions',q:'Dissolving a nonvolatile solute in a liquid solvent causes the vapor pressure above the liquid to _____',choices:['remain unchanged','increase','double','decrease'],ans:3,exp:'Nonvolatile solute particles lower the solvent\'s mole fraction, and so fewer solvent molecules escape the surface; by Raoult\'s law the vapor pressure **decreases**. The lower vapor pressure is why the boiling point rises.'},
  {s:'Solutions',q:'_____ is the concentration of all dissolved particles (osmoles) per liter of solution',choices:['Osmolarity','Molality','Normality','Molarity'],ans:0,exp:'**Osmolarity** = i × molarity: it counts every dissolved particle, so 1 M NaCl is about 2 osm/L. Osmolarity differences drive osmosis across membranes; molarity counts only formula units of solute.'},
  {s:'Acids and Bases',q:'An Arrhenius acid is a substance that _____',choices:['produces OH⁻ in water','accepts H⁺','accepts an electron pair','produces H⁺ in water'],ans:3,exp:'The **Arrhenius** definition is the narrowest: an acid increases [H⁺] (H₃O⁺) when dissolved in water, and a base increases [OH⁻]. The Brønsted-Lowry and Lewis definitions extend the idea beyond water.'},
  {s:'Acids and Bases',q:'An Arrhenius base is a substance that _____',choices:['produces OH⁻ in water','produces H⁺ in water','accepts an electron pair','accepts OH⁻'],ans:0,exp:'An **Arrhenius base** increases [OH⁻] in water, e.g., NaOH → Na⁺ + OH⁻. NH₃ is a base by the Brønsted-Lowry definition but fits the Arrhenius definition only indirectly, which is why the broader definitions are more useful.'},
  {s:'Acids and Bases',q:'A Brønsted-Lowry acid is a substance that _____',choices:['accepts H⁺','donates H⁺','produces OH⁻ in water','accepts an electron pair'],ans:1,exp:'A **Brønsted-Lowry acid** is a proton (H⁺) donor; the species left behind is its conjugate base. Accepting an electron pair is the Lewis acid definition.'},
  {s:'Acids and Bases',q:'A Brønsted-Lowry base is a substance that _____',choices:['donates H⁺','accepts H⁺','produces H⁺ in water','accepts an electron pair'],ans:1,exp:'A **Brønsted-Lowry base** is a proton (H⁺) acceptor; it uses a lone pair to bond the proton, forming its conjugate acid (e.g., NH₃ + H⁺ → NH₄⁺).'},
  {s:'Acids and Bases',q:'A Lewis acid is a substance that _____',choices:['donates OH⁻','donates an electron pair','accepts an electron pair','accepts H⁺'],ans:2,exp:'A **Lewis acid** is an electron-pair acceptor (an electrophile), e.g., BF₃, AlCl₃ or a metal cation in a complex ion. Accepting H⁺ describes a Brønsted-Lowry base.'},
  {s:'Acids and Bases',q:'A Lewis base is a substance that _____',choices:['accepts an electron pair','accepts OH⁻','donates H⁺','donates an electron pair'],ans:3,exp:'A **Lewis base** is an electron-pair donor (a nucleophile), e.g., NH₃, H₂O or a ligand binding a metal ion. Lewis acid-base reactions form coordinate covalent bonds.'},
  {s:'Acids and Bases',q:'A/an _____ is a species that can behave as an acid or a base',choices:['amphoteric species','strong electrolyte','polyprotic acid','spectator ion'],ans:0,exp:'An **amphoteric** species can act as an acid or a base depending on its partner, e.g., water, HCO₃⁻ and amino acids. Amphiprotic species (which can donate or accept H⁺) are a subset.'},
  {s:'Acids and Bases',q:'_____ are acids that can lose more than one proton per molecule',choices:['Polyprotic acids','Monoprotic acids','Amphoteric species','Strong acids'],ans:0,exp:'**Polyprotic acids** (e.g., H₂SO₄, H₃PO₄, H₂CO₃) donate protons one at a time, and each successive Ka is smaller (Ka1 > Ka2 > Ka3). Their titration curves show one equivalence point per acidic proton.'},
  {s:'Acids and Bases',q:'The equilibrium constant for the autoionization of water, Kw, is _____ at 25 °C',choices:['1 × 10⁻¹²','1 × 10⁻⁷','1 × 10⁻¹','1 × 10⁻¹⁴'],ans:3,exp:'Kw = [H⁺][OH⁻] = 1 × 10⁻¹⁴ at 25 °C, so in pure water [H⁺] = [OH⁻] = 10⁻⁷ M. Kw is temperature dependent: autoionization is endothermic, so Kw rises with temperature.'},
  {s:'Acids and Bases: pH and pKa',q:'At 25 °C, pH + pOH = _____',choices:['14','7','10','12'],ans:0,exp:'Taking −log of Kw = [H⁺][OH⁻] = 10⁻¹⁴ gives pH + pOH = pKw = **14** at 25 °C. A neutral solution has pH = pOH = 7, which is the tempting but wrong sum.'},
  {s:'Acids and Bases: pH and pKa',q:'[OH⁻] = _____',choices:['10^(−pOH)','−log[OH⁻]','10^(pOH)','14 − pH'],ans:0,exp:'Since pOH = −log[OH⁻], undoing the log gives [OH⁻] = 10^(−pOH); e.g., pOH 3 means [OH⁻] = 10⁻³ M. The expression 14 − pH equals pOH, not [OH⁻].'},
  {s:'Acids and Bases',q:'Strong acids and bases dissociate _____',choices:['not at all','reversibly','completely','partially'],ans:2,exp:'Strong acids (HCl, HBr, HI, HNO₃, HClO₄, H₂SO₄) and strong bases (Group 1 hydroxides, heavier Group 2 hydroxides) dissociate **completely** in water, so [H⁺] or [OH⁻] equals the starting concentration. Weak acids and bases dissociate only partially.'},
  {s:'Acids and Bases',q:'Weak acids and bases do not completely _____ in water',choices:['dissolve','dissociate','react','precipitate'],ans:1,exp:'Weak acids and bases only partially **dissociate**, establishing an equilibrium described by Ka or Kb. Many weak acids (e.g., acetic acid) dissolve completely, so dissolving and dissociating are not the same thing.'},
  {s:'Acids and Bases',q:'_____ is the equilibrium constant for the dissociation of an acid, HA, into a proton and its conjugate base',choices:['Keq','Ka','Kb','Kw'],ans:1,exp:'**Ka** = [H⁺][A⁻]/[HA]. A larger Ka (smaller pKa) means a stronger acid.'},
  {s:'Acids and Bases',q:'_____ is the equilibrium constant for the dissociation of a base, BOH, into a hydroxide ion and its conjugate acid',choices:['Keq','Kb','Ka','Kw'],ans:1,exp:'**Kb** = [B⁺][OH⁻]/[BOH] (or [BH⁺][OH⁻]/[B] for a base such as NH₃). For a conjugate pair, Ka × Kb = Kw.'},
  {s:'Acids and Bases: pH and pKa',q:'pKa + pKb = _____ = _____',choices:['pKw / 14','pKw / 7','Kw / 10⁻¹⁴','pH / 7'],ans:0,exp:'For a conjugate acid-base pair, Ka × Kb = Kw; taking −log of both sides gives pKa + pKb = pKw = 14 at 25 °C. So a stronger acid (low pKa) has a weaker conjugate base (high pKb).'},
  {s:'Acids and Bases: Buffers and Titrations',q:'Strong acids and bases have conjugates that are _____',choices:['negligibly weak','moderately strong','strong','amphoteric'],ans:0,exp:'Because Ka × Kb = Kw, a very large Ka forces a negligible Kb: the conjugate base of a strong acid (e.g., Cl⁻) is so weak that it does not affect pH. That is why salts such as NaCl are neutral.'},
  {s:'Acids and Bases: Buffers and Titrations',q:'Weak acids and bases have conjugates that are _____',choices:['weak','strong','negligibly weak','amphoteric'],ans:0,exp:'A weak acid has a **weak** conjugate base that still reacts measurably with water (e.g., acetate makes sodium acetate solutions basic). This is what lets a weak acid and its conjugate base act as a buffer.'},
  {s:'Acids and Bases: Buffers and Titrations',q:'A neutralization reaction is when an acid and a base react to form _____ and _____',choices:['H₂ / water','CO₂ / a salt','H₂ / a salt','a salt / water'],ans:3,exp:'In neutralization, H⁺ from the acid and OH⁻ from the base combine to form water, and the remaining cation and anion make up a salt (HCl + NaOH → NaCl + H₂O). H₂ gas comes from acids reacting with active metals, not bases.'},
  {s:'Acids and Bases: Buffers and Titrations',q:'An acidic buffer is a mixture of a _____ and _____',choices:['weak acid / its conjugate base (salt)','strong acid / strong base','weak base / its conjugate acid (salt)','strong acid / its conjugate base'],ans:0,exp:'An acidic buffer holds a weak acid (HA) to neutralize added base and its conjugate base (A⁻, usually as a salt) to neutralize added acid, e.g., acetic acid/sodium acetate. A strong acid\'s conjugate base is too weak to buffer.'},
  {s:'Acids and Bases: Buffers and Titrations',q:'A basic buffer is a mixture of a _____ and _____',choices:['weak acid / its conjugate base (salt)','strong base / strong acid','strong base / its conjugate acid','weak base / its conjugate acid (salt)'],ans:3,exp:'A basic buffer combines a weak base (e.g., NH₃) with its conjugate acid (NH₄⁺, from a salt such as NH₄Cl), so it can neutralize both added acid and added base. Its pH sits near the pKa of the conjugate acid.'},
  {s:'Acids and Bases: Buffers and Titrations',q:'Buffering capacity is a measure of the ability of a buffer to _____',choices:['raise the pH','lower the pH','resist changes in pH','hold the pH at exactly 7'],ans:2,exp:'**Buffering capacity** is how much acid or base a buffer can absorb before its pH changes significantly. It is greatest when [HA] ≈ [A⁻] (pH ≈ pKa) and when the buffer components are concentrated.'},
  {s:'Acids and Bases: Buffers and Titrations',q:'When [A⁻] = [HA] at the half-equivalence point, log(1) = _____, so pH = _____',choices:['0 / pKa','1 / pKa','0 / 7','1 / pKb'],ans:0,exp:'Henderson–Hasselbalch: pH = pKa + log([A⁻]/[HA]). At half-equivalence [A⁻] = [HA], so log(1) = 0 and **pH = pKa**, which is how pKa is read off a titration curve.'},
  {s:'Acids and Bases: Buffers and Titrations',q:'The _____, also known as the _____, is the point in the buffering region at which half of the analyte has been protonated or deprotonated',choices:['half-equivalence point / midpoint','equivalence point / endpoint','neutralization point / endpoint','equivalence point / midpoint'],ans:0,exp:'At the **half-equivalence point (midpoint)** half of the analyte (e.g., HA) has been converted to its conjugate form, so [HA] = [A⁻] and pH = pKa. The titrant is the solution being added, not the species being converted.'},
  {s:'Acids and Bases: Buffers and Titrations',q:'The _____ is the point at which equivalent amounts of acid and base have reacted',choices:['neutralization point','equivalence point','half-equivalence point','endpoint'],ans:1,exp:'At the **equivalence point**, moles of added titrant equal the moles of analyte (in equivalents), shown by the steep jump on the curve. The endpoint is where the indicator changes color, which only approximates the equivalence point.'},
  {s:'Acids and Bases: Buffers and Titrations',q:'In a titration, the _____ (the known solution) is added from a burette to a measured quantity of the _____ (the unknown solution) until the reaction is complete',choices:['titrant / analyte','analyte / titrant','indicator / titrant','analyte / indicator'],ans:0,exp:'The **titrant** has a known concentration and is delivered from the burette; the **analyte** (titrand) is the unknown in the flask. At equivalence, the known moles of titrant let you calculate the analyte\'s concentration.'},
  {s:'Acids and Bases: Buffers and Titrations',q:'Which titration curve shows an S-shaped curve with the equivalence point at pH 7 and no buffering region?',choices:['Weak acid with strong base','Strong acid with strong base','Weak acid with weak base','Strong acid with weak base'],ans:1,exp:'In a **strong acid–strong base** titration both species dissociate completely, so there is no weak acid or base to buffer and the salt formed is neutral (equivalence at pH 7). The curve rises very steeply around equivalence.'},
  {s:'Acids and Bases: Buffers and Titrations',q:'In which titration does the equivalence point fall below pH 7, with the salt formed acting as a weak acid?',choices:['Weak acid with strong base','Strong acid with strong base','Strong acid with weak base','Weak acid with weak base'],ans:2,exp:'In a **strong acid–weak base** titration (e.g., HCl into NH₃), the conjugate acid formed (NH₄⁺) donates protons to water, so the equivalence point is acidic (pH < 7). Methyl orange suits this kind of titration.'},
  {s:'Acids and Bases: Buffers and Titrations',q:'In which titration does the equivalence point fall above pH 7, with a distinct buffering region centered on the half-equivalence point (pH = pKa)?',choices:['Strong acid with strong base','Weak acid with strong base','Strong acid with weak base','Weak acid with weak base'],ans:1,exp:'In a **weak acid–strong base** titration, the conjugate base (e.g., acetate) accepts protons from water, so the equivalence point is basic (pH > 7). Before equivalence, the HA/A⁻ mixture buffers around pH = pKa; phenolphthalein is a suitable indicator.'},
  {s:'Acids and Bases: Buffers and Titrations',q:'Which titration produces the least dramatic pH change at the equivalence point and has the most gradual S-curve?',choices:['Strong acid with strong base','Weak acid with strong base','Strong acid with weak base','Weak acid with weak base'],ans:3,exp:'In a **weak acid–weak base** titration both conjugates react with water and partly buffer the equivalence point, so the pH jump is small and gradual. The equivalence pH depends on the relative Ka and Kb, which makes indicators hard to use.'},
  {s:'Acids and Bases: Buffers and Titrations',q:'_____ are weak acids or bases that display different colors in the protonated and deprotonated forms',choices:['Titrants','Electrolytes','Indicators','Buffers'],ans:2,exp:'**Indicators** (HIn ⇌ H⁺ + In⁻) change color over a range of about pKa(HIn) ± 1. They are added in tiny amounts so that they do not consume significant titrant.'},
  {s:'Acids and Bases: Buffers and Titrations',q:'When choosing a titration indicator, you want one that changes color in a range close to the _____',choices:['neutralization point','half-equivalence point','pKa of the analyte','equivalence point'],ans:3,exp:'An indicator should change color (its endpoint) as close as possible to the **equivalence point**, so its pKa should be near the equivalence pH. The pKa of the analyte marks the half-equivalence point, which is the wrong place to stop.'},
  {s:'Acids and Bases: Buffers and Titrations',q:'In an acid, _____ litmus paper shows no color change, while _____ litmus paper turns _____',choices:['red / blue / red','red / red / blue','blue / red / blue','blue / blue / red'],ans:0,exp:'Acid turns blue litmus **red**, so red litmus stays red while blue litmus turns red. Mnemonic: Blue to Red = Acid ("BRA").'},
  {s:'Acids and Bases: Buffers and Titrations',q:'In a base, _____ litmus paper shows no color change, while _____ litmus paper turns _____',choices:['red / blue / red','red / red / blue','blue / blue / red','blue / red / blue'],ans:3,exp:'Base turns red litmus **blue**, so blue litmus stays blue while red litmus turns blue. It is the mirror image of the acid case (Blue to Red = Acid).'},
  {s:'Acids and Bases: Buffers and Titrations',q:'In a/an _____ solution, both red and blue litmus paper show no change in color',choices:['basic','amphoteric','acidic','neutral'],ans:3,exp:'Litmus changes color around pH 4.5–8.3, so in a **neutral** solution neither red nor blue litmus changes. An acid turns blue litmus red and a base turns red litmus blue.'},
  {s:'Acids and Bases: Buffers and Titrations',q:'Universal indicator paper turns _____ in acids, _____ in neutral solutions and _____ in alkalis',choices:['red to yellow / green / blue to purple','blue to purple / green / red to yellow','green / red to yellow / blue to purple','red to yellow / colorless / pink'],ans:0,exp:'Universal indicator mixes several indicators to give a color spectrum: red through yellow for acidic pH, **green** near pH 7, and blue to purple for basic pH. Colorless-to-pink is the behavior of phenolphthalein, not universal indicator.'},
  {s:'Acids and Bases: Buffers and Titrations',q:'Phenolphthalein is colorless below a pH of about _____ and turns pink above it',choices:['8.2','7.0','10.0','6.0'],ans:0,exp:'Phenolphthalein changes from colorless to pink over about pH **8.2–10**, which makes it ideal for weak acid–strong base titrations with a basic equivalence point. It does not change color at pH 7.'},
  {s:'Acids and Bases: Buffers and Titrations',q:'Methyl orange is red at a pH less than about _____ and is yellow at a pH greater than about _____',choices:['3.1 / 4.4','4.4 / 3.1','4.0 / 6.0','2.0 / 3.5'],ans:0,exp:'Methyl orange is red below pH ~3.1 and yellow above ~4.4 (orange in between), so it suits titrations with an acidic equivalence point (strong acid + weak base).'},
  {s:'Acids and Bases: Buffers and Titrations',q:'Bromothymol blue is yellow up to a pH of about _____ and is blue at any pH greater than about _____',choices:['6.0 / 7.6','3.0 / 4.6','8.2 / 10.0','3.1 / 4.4'],ans:0,exp:'Bromothymol blue changes from yellow (pH < 6.0) to blue (pH > 7.6), which suits strong acid–strong base titrations with an equivalence point near 7. Bromophenol blue changes at 3.0–4.6, and phenolphthalein at 8.2–10.'},
  {s:'Acids and Bases: Buffers and Titrations',q:'The endpoint of a titration is when _____',choices:['the indicator changes color','the analyte is fully consumed','the pH equals 7','the titrant volume equals the analyte volume'],ans:0,exp:'The **endpoint** is the observable signal, the indicator\'s color change. The moment the analyte is fully consumed is the equivalence point; a well-chosen indicator makes the two nearly coincide.'},
  {s:'Acids and Bases: Buffers and Titrations',q:'Titrations of a _____ show multiple buffering regions and equivalence points',choices:['polyprotic acid','monoprotic weak acid','monoprotic strong acid','strong base'],ans:0,exp:'A **polyprotic** (polyvalent) acid such as H₃PO₄ loses its protons one at a time, so the curve has one buffering region and one equivalence point per ionizable proton. Monoprotic acids give a single equivalence point.'},
  {s:'Acids and Bases: Buffers and Titrations',q:'At the half-equivalence point (midpoint) of a titration, pH = _____',choices:['7','pKb','pKw','pKa'],ans:3,exp:'At the half-equivalence point [HA] = [A⁻], so the Henderson–Hasselbalch log term is 0 and **pH = pKa**. A pH of 7 is expected only at the equivalence point of a strong acid–strong base titration.'},
  {s:'REDOX',q:'Any free element or diatomic species has an oxidation number of _____',choices:['0','−1','+1','its group number'],ans:0,exp:'Atoms in their elemental form (Na, Fe, O₂, Cl₂, P₄) have an oxidation number of **0** because the electrons are shared equally or not transferred at all.'},
  {s:'REDOX',q:'A monatomic ion has an oxidation number equal to _____',choices:['zero','its ionic charge','its atomic number','its group number'],ans:1,exp:'For a monatomic ion, the oxidation number equals **the charge of the ion** (Fe³⁺ is +3, Cl⁻ is −1). The group number only predicts the usual charge for main-group elements.'},
  {s:'REDOX',q:'When in a compound, metals have the following oxidation states: Group 1A metals = _____ and Group 2A metals = _____',choices:['+1 / +2','+2 / +1','+1 / +3','+2 / +3'],ans:0,exp:'Group 1A metals lose their single valence electron (+1) and Group 2A metals lose their two valence electrons (+2) in compounds. Their low ionization energies make these the only common oxidation states.'},
  {s:'REDOX',q:'When in a compound, group 7A elements (halogens) have an oxidation state of _____ unless combined with an element of greater electronegativity',choices:['−2','−1','0','+1'],ans:1,exp:'Halogens usually take **−1** because they are highly electronegative and need one electron to fill their octet. Bonded to a more electronegative atom (O, or a lighter halogen), Cl, Br and I can be positive, as in ClO⁻; fluorine is always −1.'},
  {s:'REDOX',q:'The oxidation state of hydrogen in a compound is _____, except in metal hydrides such as NaH and LiH, in which the oxidation state of H is _____',choices:['+1 / −1','+1 / 0','0 / −1','−1 / +1'],ans:0,exp:'Hydrogen is usually **+1**, but in metal hydrides it is bonded to a less electronegative metal and is **−1** (the hydride ion, H⁻). Hydride reagents such as NaBH₄ and LiAlH₄ act as reducing agents for this reason.'},
  {s:'REDOX',q:'The oxidation state of oxygen in a compound is _____, except in peroxides such as H₂O₂ and Na₂O₂, in which the oxidation state of O is _____',choices:['−2 / −1','−1 / −2','−2 / −2','0 / −1'],ans:0,exp:'Oxygen is usually **−2**, but in peroxides (O–O bond) each oxygen is **−1**. OF₂ is a further exception, where O is +2 because fluorine is more electronegative.'},
  {s:'REDOX',q:'The sum of the oxidation numbers in a neutral compound is _____',choices:['the atomic number','the charge of each ion','zero','one'],ans:2,exp:'The oxidation numbers in a neutral compound must add up to **zero**, e.g., in H₂SO₄: 2(+1) + S + 4(−2) = 0, so S = +6. This rule lets you solve for an unknown oxidation state.'},
  {s:'REDOX',q:'The sum of the oxidation numbers in a polyatomic ion is equal to _____',choices:['the number of atoms','the ion\'s overall charge','zero','the central atom\'s charge'],ans:1,exp:'In a polyatomic ion, the oxidation numbers add up to **the ion\'s overall charge**, e.g., in MnO₄⁻: Mn + 4(−2) = −1, so Mn = +7.'},
  {s:'REDOX',q:'The _____ shows all of the ions present in a reaction, including spectator ions',choices:['molecular equation','complete ionic equation','net ionic equation','balanced equation'],ans:1,exp:'The **complete ionic equation** writes every strong electrolyte as separate ions, including spectator ions that do not react. Cancelling the spectators gives the net ionic equation.'},
  {s:'REDOX',q:'The _____ is a chemical equation for a reaction that lists only the species participating in the reaction',choices:['net ionic equation','skeleton equation','complete ionic equation','molecular equation'],ans:0,exp:'The **net ionic equation** removes spectator ions and keeps only the species that change, e.g., Ag⁺ + Cl⁻ → AgCl(s). The complete ionic equation still includes the spectators.'},
  {s:'REDOX',q:'_____, also known as _____, is a redox reaction in which a single species is simultaneously oxidized and reduced, forming two different products',choices:['Disproportionation / dismutation','Comproportionation / synproportionation','Decomposition / dissociation','Hydrolysis / solvolysis'],ans:0,exp:'In **disproportionation (dismutation)**, one element in one species goes to both a higher and a lower oxidation state, e.g., 2H₂O₂ → 2H₂O + O₂, or superoxide dismutase converting O₂⁻ to O₂ and H₂O₂. Comproportionation is the reverse.'},
  {s:'REDOX',q:'A _____ is similar in method to an acid–base titration, but it follows the transfer of electrons',choices:['complexometric titration','back titration','redox titration','precipitation titration'],ans:2,exp:'A **redox titration** uses an oxidizing or reducing titrant (e.g., KMnO₄, which is also its own purple indicator) and tracks electron transfer instead of proton transfer. The equivalence point is where moles of electrons given equal moles accepted.'},
  {s:'Electrochemistry',q:'In an electrochemical cell, the anode attracts _____ and is always the site of _____',choices:['anions / oxidation','anions / reduction','cations / reduction','cations / oxidation'],ans:0,exp:'Oxidation always occurs at the anode ("AN OX"). Anions migrate toward the anode to balance the positive charge that builds up there as the electrode is oxidized.'},
  {s:'Electrochemistry',q:'In an electrochemical cell, the cathode attracts _____ and is always the site of _____',choices:['cations / reduction','cations / oxidation','anions / oxidation','anions / reduction'],ans:0,exp:'Reduction always occurs at the cathode ("RED CAT"), and cations migrate toward it. This holds for both galvanic and electrolytic cells; only the electrode signs differ.'},
  {s:'Electrochemistry',q:'In an electrochemical cell, electrons flow from the _____ to the _____',choices:['anode / anode','anode / cathode','cathode / cathode','cathode / anode'],ans:1,exp:'Electrons are released by oxidation at the **anode** and travel through the external wire to the **cathode**, where reduction consumes them. Alphabetical mnemonic: electrons flow from A to C.'},
  {s:'Electrochemistry',q:'In an electrochemical cell, conventional current flows from the _____ to the _____',choices:['cathode / cathode','anode / anode','anode / cathode','cathode / anode'],ans:3,exp:'Conventional current is the flow of positive charge, opposite to electron flow, so in the external circuit it goes from the **cathode** to the **anode**.'},
  {s:'Electrochemistry',q:'Galvanic cells are also known as _____',choices:['voltaic cells','electrolytic cells','concentration cells','fuel cells'],ans:0,exp:'**Galvanic (voltaic) cells** convert the free energy of a spontaneous redox reaction into electrical work, as in an ordinary battery. A concentration cell is one type of galvanic cell, not another name for all of them.'},
  {s:'Electrochemistry',q:'Galvanic cells house _____ reactions',choices:['endothermic','spontaneous','reversible','non-spontaneous'],ans:1,exp:'Galvanic cells run **spontaneous** reactions: E°cell > 0 and ΔG° < 0 (ΔG° = −nFE°cell). Electrolytic cells need an external power source to drive non-spontaneous reactions.'},
  {s:'Electrochemistry',q:'Electrolytic cells house _____ reactions',choices:['reversible','exothermic','spontaneous','non-spontaneous'],ans:3,exp:'Electrolytic cells use an external voltage to drive **non-spontaneous** reactions (E°cell < 0, ΔG° > 0), e.g., electrolysis of water or electroplating. In them the anode is positive and the cathode negative, the reverse of galvanic cells.'},
  {s:'Electrochemistry',q:'A _____ is a galvanic cell in which both half-cells have identical electrodes and electrolytes that differ only in concentration',choices:['fuel cell','rechargeable cell','concentration cell','electrolytic cell'],ans:2,exp:'In a **concentration cell** E° = 0, but the concentration difference makes E = −(RT/nF)lnQ positive, so current flows spontaneously until both sides reach equal concentration. The resting membrane potential of neurons is modeled the same way.'},
  {s:'Electrochemistry',q:'_____ is a measure of the tendency of a chemical species to gain electrons and be reduced',choices:['Reduction potential','Electromotive force','Oxidation potential','Electron affinity'],ans:0,exp:'A more positive **reduction potential** means a species is more easily reduced, so it is a stronger oxidizing agent. The oxidation potential is the same value with the opposite sign.'},
  {s:'Electrochemistry',q:'The _____ is the tendency of a chemical species to be reduced, measured in volts under standard conditions',choices:['standard reduction potential','electromotive force','standard oxidation potential','standard cell potential'],ans:0,exp:'The **standard reduction potential (E°red)** is measured against the standard hydrogen electrode (0 V) at 298 K, 1 atm and 1 M. E°cell is then E°red(cathode) − E°red(anode).'},
  {s:'Electrochemistry',q:'The _____ is the maximum potential difference between the two electrodes of a galvanic or voltaic cell',choices:['oxidation potential','overpotential','reduction potential','electromotive force'],ans:3,exp:'The **electromotive force (EMF)** is the cell voltage when no current is drawn, equal to E°cell under standard conditions. A positive EMF corresponds to a spontaneous (galvanic) cell.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'_____ are hydrocarbons in which all carbon–carbon bonds are single bonds',choices:['Alkenes','Alkanes','Alkynes','Arenes'],ans:1,exp:'**Alkanes** are saturated hydrocarbons (CₙH₂ₙ₊₂ for acyclic chains) with only sp³ carbons and C–C single bonds. Alkenes, alkynes and arenes all contain π bonds.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'Alkanes are named according to the number of _____ present, followed by the suffix _____',choices:['hydrogens / –ane','carbons / –yne','carbons / –ane','carbons / –ene'],ans:2,exp:'Alkanes are named by the number of carbons in the parent chain (meth-, eth-, prop-, but-, pent-...) plus the suffix **–ane**, which marks all single bonds.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'Alkenes have a _____ bond and use the suffix _____',choices:['double / –al','double / –ene','single / –ane','triple / –yne'],ans:1,exp:'Alkenes contain at least one C=C **double** bond (one σ + one π) and use the suffix **–ene** (e.g., ethene). The suffix –al marks an aldehyde, not an alkene.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'Alkynes have a _____ bond and use the suffix _____',choices:['triple / –al','triple / –yne','single / –ane','double / –ene'],ans:1,exp:'Alkynes contain a C≡C **triple** bond (one σ + two π, sp carbons) and use the suffix **–yne** (e.g., ethyne, also called acetylene).'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'Alcohols have a/an _____ group and use the suffix _____ or the prefix _____',choices:['–COOH / –oic acid / carboxy-','–NH₂ / –amine / amino-','–OH / –ol / hydroxy-','–CHO / –al / oxo-'],ans:2,exp:'Alcohols contain a hydroxyl (**–OH**) group. They take the suffix **–ol** when the alcohol is the highest-priority group, and the prefix **hydroxy-** when a higher-priority group is present.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'Diols contain two _____ groups',choices:['amino','thiol','hydroxyl','carbonyl'],ans:2,exp:'A **diol** (glycol) has two –OH (hydroxyl) groups, e.g., ethylene glycol. Diols are geminal if both –OH groups are on the same carbon and vicinal if on adjacent carbons.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'A carbon atom double-bonded to an oxygen atom (C=O) is called a/an _____',choices:['carbonyl group','amino group','carboxyl group','hydroxyl group'],ans:0,exp:'The **carbonyl** group (C=O) is found in aldehydes, ketones, carboxylic acids and their derivatives; its carbon is electrophilic because oxygen pulls electron density away. A carboxyl group is a carbonyl plus an –OH on the same carbon.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'Aldehydes have a carbonyl group on the _____ carbon',choices:['terminal','internal','alpha','aromatic'],ans:0,exp:'In an aldehyde the carbonyl carbon is bonded to at least one hydrogen (R–CHO), so it must be at the **terminal** end of a chain (C-1). The alpha carbon is the one next to the carbonyl.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'Ketones have a carbonyl group on a/an _____ carbon',choices:['terminal','internal','alpha','aromatic'],ans:1,exp:'In a ketone the carbonyl carbon is bonded to two carbon groups, so it sits on an **internal** (non-terminal) carbon, never at the end of the chain. A carbonyl at the end of a chain would be an aldehyde.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'Alcohols are classified as 1°, 2° and 3° according to the number of carbons attached to the _____',choices:['carbon bearing the –OH','carbon next to the –OH','oxygen atom','terminal carbon'],ans:0,exp:'The class depends on how many carbons are bonded to the **carbinol carbon** (the C bearing –OH): one = 1°, two = 2°, three = 3°. This matters for oxidation: 3° alcohols cannot be oxidized because that carbon has no H.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'Amines are classified as 1°, 2° and 3° according to the number of carbons attached to the _____',choices:['nitrogen atom','alpha carbon','carbon next to the nitrogen','most substituted carbon'],ans:0,exp:'Amine class counts the carbon groups bonded directly to the **nitrogen** (RNH₂ = 1°, R₂NH = 2°, R₃N = 3°). This differs from alcohols, whose class is based on the carbon bearing the –OH.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'_____ is the highest priority functional group',choices:['Ester','Amide','Anhydride','Carboxylic acid'],ans:3,exp:'In IUPAC naming the **carboxylic acid** outranks its derivatives (anhydride > ester > amide), followed by aldehydes, ketones, alcohols and amines. Priority tracks oxidation state: the carboxyl carbon has three bonds to oxygen.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'_____ have the same molecular formula but a different arrangement of atoms (in connectivity or in space)',choices:['Isotopes','Diastereomers','Isomers','Enantiomers'],ans:2,exp:'**Isomers** share a molecular formula. Constitutional isomers differ in connectivity, and stereoisomers (enantiomers, diastereomers) share connectivity but differ in 3D arrangement, so enantiomers and diastereomers are subtypes rather than the general term.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'_____ have identical molecular formulas but differ in the connectivity of their atoms',choices:['Stereoisomers','Conformational isomers','Constitutional isomers','Enantiomers'],ans:2,exp:'**Constitutional (structural) isomers** share a molecular formula but have atoms bonded in a different order, e.g. butane vs. isobutane, so they often have very different physical properties. Stereoisomers, including enantiomers and conformers, keep the same connectivity and differ only in 3D arrangement.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'The general term for isomers that share the same atomic connectivity but differ in the 3D arrangement of their atoms is _____',choices:['stereoisomers','constitutional isomers','tautomers','resonance structures'],ans:0,exp:'**Stereoisomers** have the same connectivity but a different spatial arrangement; enantiomers, diastereomers and conformers are all subtypes. Tautomers (e.g., keto/enol) differ in connectivity, and resonance structures are not isomers at all, just different electron depictions of one molecule.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'A carbon atom bonded to four different groups is called a/an _____',choices:['quaternary carbon','chiral center','prochiral center','achiral center'],ans:1,exp:'A carbon with four different substituents is a **chiral center** (stereogenic center): swapping any two groups gives the other configuration (R ↔ S). A quaternary carbon is bonded to four carbons, which need not be different, and a prochiral carbon becomes chiral only after one more change.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'For a molecule with n chiral centers, the maximum number of possible stereoisomers is _____',choices:['2n','2ⁿ','n²','2ⁿ⁻¹'],ans:1,exp:'Each chiral center can be R or S, so n centers give at most **2ⁿ** stereoisomers (e.g., 4 for two centers, 8 for three). Meso compounds lower the actual count; 2ⁿ⁻¹ is the number of enantiomer pairs, not the number of stereoisomers.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'_____ differ only by rotation around a single σ bond',choices:['Conformational isomers','Diastereomers','Enantiomers','Constitutional isomers'],ans:0,exp:'**Conformational isomers** (conformers) interconvert by rotation about σ bonds without breaking any bonds, as in the staggered and eclipsed forms of ethane or the chair flip of cyclohexane. Enantiomers and diastereomers are configurational isomers: converting one to another requires breaking bonds.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'In a Newman projection of butane, the staggered conformation in which the two methyl groups are 180° apart is the _____ conformation',choices:['gauche','anti','eclipsed','totally eclipsed'],ans:1,exp:'The **anti** conformation places the two CH₃ groups 180° apart, minimizing both torsional and steric strain, so it is butane\'s lowest-energy conformer. Gauche is also staggered but has the methyls 60° apart (higher energy); totally eclipsed (0°) is the highest-energy form.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'A Newman projection in which the front-carbon substituents directly overlap the back-carbon substituents shows the _____ conformation',choices:['gauche','staggered','anti','eclipsed'],ans:3,exp:'In the **eclipsed** conformation the front and back bonds line up (0° dihedral angle), maximizing torsional strain, so it is the highest-energy conformation. Staggered conformations (anti and gauche) offset the bonds by 60°.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'In the chair form of cyclohexane, a bond that points outward from the ring, roughly perpendicular to the ring\'s axis, is in the _____ position',choices:['axial','equatorial','anti','gauche'],ans:1,exp:'**Equatorial** bonds point out around the "equator" of the ring, roughly perpendicular to its axis. Bulky substituents prefer equatorial positions because axial groups suffer 1,3-diaxial steric strain; a ring flip interconverts axial and equatorial.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'In the chair form of cyclohexane, a bond that points straight up or down, parallel to the ring\'s axis, is in the _____ position',choices:['axial','equatorial','anti','gauche'],ans:0,exp:'**Axial** bonds run parallel to the ring\'s axis, alternating up and down on adjacent carbons. Large groups in axial positions clash with the other axial hydrogens on the same side (1,3-diaxial interactions), so the equatorial conformer is usually favored.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'The two categories of _____ are enantiomers and diastereomers',choices:['geometric isomers','conformational isomers','constitutional isomers','configurational isomers'],ans:3,exp:'**Configurational isomers** can only be interconverted by breaking bonds; they are divided into enantiomers (nonsuperimposable mirror images) and diastereomers (stereoisomers that are not mirror images). Geometric (cis/trans) isomers are just one kind of diastereomer, and conformational isomers interconvert by bond rotation.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'Optical activity is the ability of a molecule to _____ plane-polarized light',choices:['reflect','absorb','transmit','rotate'],ans:3,exp:'Chiral molecules **rotate** the plane of plane-polarized light: (+)/dextrorotatory to the right, (−)/levorotatory to the left. The direction of rotation must be measured with a polarimeter and cannot be predicted from the R/S configuration.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'A 50:50 mixture of two enantiomers is called a/an _____ mixture',choices:['meso','racemic','enantiopure','diastereomeric'],ans:1,exp:'In a **racemic** mixture the equal and opposite rotations of the two enantiomers cancel, so it is optically inactive. A meso compound is also optically inactive, but it is a single achiral compound, not a mixture.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'_____ contain chiral centers but have an internal plane of symmetry, so they are optically inactive',choices:['Diastereomers','Racemic mixtures','Enantiomers','Meso compounds'],ans:3,exp:'**Meso compounds** (e.g., meso-tartaric acid) have chiral centers whose rotations cancel internally because of a mirror plane, so the molecule is achiral and superimposable on its mirror image. A racemic mixture is also optically inactive, but because it contains two separate enantiomers.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'Cis groups are on _____ side(s) of a double bond or ring; trans groups are on _____ side(s)',choices:['the same / opposite','opposite / the same','the same / the same','opposite / opposite'],ans:0,exp:'**Cis** groups lie on the same side of a double bond or ring and **trans** groups lie on opposite sides. Cis/trans isomers are diastereomers, so they differ in physical properties such as boiling point and dipole moment.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'_____ describes a compound\'s stereochemistry by comparison with another compound, as in the D/L system based on glyceraldehyde',choices:['Optical rotation','Conformation','Absolute configuration','Relative configuration'],ans:3,exp:'**Relative configuration** relates a molecule\'s stereochemistry to a reference compound; the D/L labels for sugars and amino acids are assigned by comparison with D- and L-glyceraldehyde. D/L does not predict the sign of optical rotation (+/−), which must be measured.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'_____ describes the actual spatial arrangement of the groups at a stereocenter without comparison to any other compound',choices:['Relative configuration','Conformation','Absolute configuration','Optical rotation'],ans:2,exp:'**Absolute configuration** gives the real 3D arrangement at a stereocenter and is described with the Cahn-Ingold-Prelog R/S labels. Relative configuration (e.g., D/L) is defined only by comparison with a reference such as glyceraldehyde.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'The Cahn-Ingold-Prelog priority rules are used for _____',choices:['identifying functional groups','calculating oxidation states','naming stereoisomers','numbering the parent chain'],ans:2,exp:'CIP rules rank substituents by atomic number (heavier isotope wins ties, then the next atoms out) so stereoisomers can be named: R/S for chiral centers and E/Z for double bonds. Parent-chain numbering follows separate IUPAC rules based on lowest locants for the highest-priority group.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'In the E/Z naming system for alkenes, (Z) has the higher-priority groups on _____ side(s) of the double bond and (E) has them on _____ side(s)',choices:['opposite / the same','the same / the same','opposite / opposite','the same / opposite'],ans:3,exp:'Using CIP priorities on each alkene carbon, **Z** (zusammen, "together"; think "**Z**ame **Z**ide") has the higher-priority groups on the same side and **E** (entgegen, "opposite") has them on opposite sides. E/Z is not always the same as trans/cis, because cis/trans compares identical groups rather than priorities.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'In a Fischer projection, vertical lines point _____ the viewer and horizontal lines point _____ the viewer',choices:['away from / toward','toward / away from','away from / away from','toward / toward'],ans:0,exp:'In a **Fischer projection**, vertical bonds go back into the page (away from the viewer) and horizontal bonds come out of the page (toward the viewer), like a bow tie. Swapping any two groups inverts the configuration, while rotating the projection 180° in the plane keeps it.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'Quantum number n is the _____ quantum number and gives the electron\'s _____',choices:['principal / energy level','azimuthal / energy level','principal / orbital shape','magnetic / energy level'],ans:0,exp:'n is the **principal** quantum number (n = 1, 2, 3, …) and gives the electron\'s shell, i.e. its energy level and average distance from the nucleus. Orbital shape is set by the azimuthal number l, and orientation by mₗ.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'Quantum number l is the _____ quantum number and gives the _____ of an orbital',choices:['magnetic / shape','azimuthal / orientation','principal / size','azimuthal / shape'],ans:3,exp:'l is the **azimuthal** (angular momentum) quantum number, ranging from 0 to n − 1, and gives the subshell and orbital shape: l = 0 is s, 1 is p, 2 is d, 3 is f. Orientation in space is given by the magnetic quantum number mₗ.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'Quantum number mₗ is the _____ quantum number and gives the orbital\'s _____',choices:['magnetic / orientation','magnetic / shape','azimuthal / orientation','spin / orientation'],ans:0,exp:'mₗ is the **magnetic** quantum number, ranging from −l to +l, and gives the orbital\'s orientation in space (e.g., px, py and pz for l = 1). The number of mₗ values, 2l + 1, is the number of orbitals in the subshell.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'Quantum number mₛ is the _____ quantum number and gives the _____ of the electron',choices:['magnetic / spin direction','spin / orientation','spin / spin direction','principal / spin direction'],ans:2,exp:'mₛ is the **spin** quantum number, +½ or −½. By the Pauli exclusion principle, two electrons sharing an orbital must have opposite spins; orbital orientation belongs to the magnetic quantum number mₗ.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'_____ orbitals are obtained by combining the _____ orbitals of the individual atoms that make up the molecule',choices:['Molecular / atomic','Atomic / molecular','Hybrid / molecular','Ionic / atomic'],ans:0,exp:'**Molecular orbitals** form from the linear combination of atomic orbitals on the bonded atoms: in-phase overlap gives bonding MOs and out-of-phase overlap gives antibonding MOs. Hybrid orbitals, by contrast, mix atomic orbitals on a single atom.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'Bonding molecular orbitals are created by overlap of atomic orbitals of _____ sign (phase)',choices:['the same','opposite','alternating','undefined'],ans:0,exp:'When atomic orbitals of **the same** sign overlap, their wavefunctions add constructively, concentrating electron density between the nuclei and lowering the energy (bonding MO). Overlap of opposite signs creates a node between the nuclei and gives a higher-energy antibonding MO.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'Antibonding molecular orbitals are created by overlap of atomic orbitals of _____ sign (phase)',choices:['the same','opposite','alternating','undefined'],ans:1,exp:'Overlap of **opposite**-sign (out-of-phase) orbitals cancels electron density between the nuclei, creating a node and an antibonding orbital (marked *) that is higher in energy than the original atomic orbitals. Bond order = ½(bonding e⁻ − antibonding e⁻).'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'Multiple bonds are _____ flexible than single bonds',choices:['more','slightly more','equally','less'],ans:3,exp:'Multiple bonds are **less** flexible because the π bond needs side-by-side p-orbital overlap, and rotation would break that overlap. That is why double bonds are rigid and give cis/trans isomers, while single (σ) bonds rotate freely.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'Compared with a single bond between the same two atoms, a double or triple bond is _____',choices:['longer and stronger','shorter and stronger','shorter and weaker','longer and weaker'],ans:1,exp:'More shared electron pairs pull the nuclei closer, so multiple bonds are **shorter and stronger** overall (C≡C < C=C < C–C in length). A single π bond is weaker than a σ bond, but the σ + π combination is still stronger than a σ bond alone.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'_____ is the model in which an atom\'s s and p orbitals mix to form a new set of equivalent orbitals used for bonding',choices:['Molecular orbital theory','Resonance','Conjugation','Orbital hybridization'],ans:3,exp:'**Orbital hybridization** mixes atomic orbitals on one atom into equivalent hybrids (sp, sp², sp³) that explain observed geometries, such as methane\'s four identical 109.5° bonds. Molecular orbital theory instead combines orbitals from different atoms across the whole molecule.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'sp³ hybridization has _____ s character and _____ p character',choices:['25% / 75%','33% / 67%','50% / 50%','75% / 25%'],ans:0,exp:'sp³ orbitals mix one s and three p orbitals, so each has 1/4 = **25% s** and 3/4 = **75% p** character. Less s character means electrons are held farther from the nucleus, so sp³ C–H bonds are the longest and least acidic.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'sp² hybridization has _____ s character and _____ p character',choices:['25% / 75%','33% / 67%','50% / 50%','67% / 33%'],ans:1,exp:'sp² orbitals mix one s and two p orbitals, giving about **33% s** and **67% p** character, with one unhybridized p orbital left to form a π bond. More s character holds electrons closer to the nucleus, giving shorter, stronger bonds.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'sp hybridization has _____ s character and _____ p character',choices:['67% / 33%','50% / 50%','33% / 67%','25% / 75%'],ans:1,exp:'sp orbitals mix one s and one p orbital, giving **50% s** and **50% p** character. The high s character stabilizes the conjugate base, which is why terminal alkyne C–H protons (pKa ≈ 25) are far more acidic than alkene or alkane C–H protons.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'_____ occurs when single and multiple bonds alternate',choices:['Aromaticity','Conjugation','Resonance','Hybridization'],ans:1,exp:'**Conjugation** is alternating single and multiple bonds (or a π bond next to a lone pair or empty p orbital), which places unhybridized p orbitals side by side so π electrons can delocalize. Aromaticity is a special case that also requires a planar, cyclic system with 4n + 2 π electrons.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'_____ is the delocalization of electrons in molecules that have conjugated bonds',choices:['Resonance','Induction','Tautomerism','Hyperconjugation'],ans:0,exp:'**Resonance** describes π electrons (and lone pairs) spread over a conjugated system; the true structure is a hybrid of the drawn resonance forms, and delocalization stabilizes it. Induction is polarization through σ bonds, and hyperconjugation involves σ bonds donating into adjacent empty or π orbitals.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'_____ acids are electron-pair acceptors',choices:['Lewis','Brønsted-Lowry','Arrhenius','Lux-Flood'],ans:0,exp:'**Lewis** acids accept an electron pair to form a coordinate covalent bond (e.g., BF₃, AlCl₃, Fe³⁺, H⁺). This is the broadest definition; Brønsted-Lowry acids are defined more narrowly as proton donors.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'_____ bases are electron-pair donors',choices:['Lewis','Lux-Flood','Brønsted-Lowry','Arrhenius'],ans:0,exp:'**Lewis** bases donate a lone pair to an electron-pair acceptor (e.g., NH₃, H₂O, Cl⁻). Every nucleophile is acting as a Lewis base; Arrhenius bases are defined narrowly as compounds that increase [OH⁻] in water.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'A/an _____ acid is a proton donor',choices:['Arrhenius','Lux-Flood','Lewis','Brønsted-Lowry'],ans:3,exp:'A **Brønsted-Lowry** acid donates H⁺ to a base. An Arrhenius acid is narrower (it must increase [H⁺] in water), and a Lewis acid is defined by accepting an electron pair rather than donating a proton.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'A/an _____ base is a proton acceptor',choices:['Arrhenius','Lux-Flood','Brønsted-Lowry','Lewis'],ans:2,exp:'A **Brønsted-Lowry** base uses a lone pair to accept H⁺. An Arrhenius base increases [OH⁻] in water, and a Lewis base donates an electron pair to any electrophile, not just a proton.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'_____ molecules can act as either acids or bases',choices:['Amphipathic','Aprotic','Neutral','Amphoteric'],ans:3,exp:'**Amphoteric** species can donate or accept protons depending on their partner: water, HCO₃⁻, H₂PO₄⁻ and amino acids are classic examples. Don\'t confuse this with amphipathic, which means having both hydrophilic and hydrophobic regions (e.g., phospholipids).'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'Kₐ is the _____ constant',choices:['base dissociation','water autoionization','solubility product','acid dissociation'],ans:3,exp:'Kₐ is the **acid dissociation** constant: Kₐ = [H⁺][A⁻]/[HA] for HA ⇌ H⁺ + A⁻. A larger Kₐ (smaller pKa) means a stronger acid; Kb is the base dissociation constant, and Kₐ × Kb = Kw for a conjugate pair.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'A lower pKa means a _____ acid',choices:['stronger','weaker','more concentrated','more dilute'],ans:0,exp:'pKa = −log Kₐ, so a lower pKa means a larger Kₐ and a **stronger** acid that dissociates more completely. Acid strength is an intrinsic property, unlike concentration, which describes how much acid is present.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'A reaction in which electrons are transferred completely from one species to another is a/an _____ reaction',choices:['acid-base','precipitation','redox','substitution'],ans:2,exp:'In a **redox** (reduction-oxidation) reaction one species loses electrons (is oxidized) and another gains them (is reduced), so oxidation numbers change. Mnemonic: OIL RIG (Oxidation Is Loss, Reduction Is Gain).'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'_____ is the charge an atom would have if all its bonds were completely ionic',choices:['Effective nuclear charge','Formal charge','Partial charge','Oxidation number'],ans:3,exp:'**Oxidation number** assigns all bonding electrons to the more electronegative atom, as if every bond were ionic. Formal charge makes the opposite assumption, splitting bonding electrons equally as if every bond were purely covalent.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'In a redox reaction, the species that loses electrons is _____',choices:['protonated','neutralized','reduced','oxidized'],ans:3,exp:'Losing electrons is **oxidation** (OIL RIG), so the species that loses electrons is oxidized and its oxidation number increases. The oxidized species is acting as the reducing agent.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'An oxidizing agent _____ electrons and is _____ in the process',choices:['accepts / oxidized','donates / oxidized','donates / reduced','accepts / reduced'],ans:3,exp:'An **oxidizing agent** oxidizes another species by taking its electrons, so it gains electrons and is itself reduced (e.g., KMnO₄, O₂). The agent is always named for what it does to the other species, not what happens to itself.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'In a redox reaction, the species that gains electrons is _____',choices:['reduced','protonated','neutralized','oxidized'],ans:0,exp:'Gaining electrons is **reduction** (RIG: Reduction Is Gain), so the oxidation number decreases ("reduces"). The reduced species is acting as the oxidizing agent.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'A reducing agent _____ electrons and is _____ in the process',choices:['donates / reduced','accepts / oxidized','accepts / reduced','donates / oxidized'],ans:3,exp:'A **reducing agent** gives electrons to another species and is itself oxidized (e.g., NaBH₄ and LiAlH₄ supply hydride, metals like Zn lose electrons). The common error is thinking a reducing agent is itself reduced.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'_____ is the preference of a reagent or intermediate to react with one functional group over another functional group in the same molecule',choices:['Stereoselectivity','Regioselectivity','Enantioselectivity','Chemoselectivity'],ans:3,exp:'**Chemoselectivity** is selectivity between different functional groups, e.g. NaBH₄ reducing a ketone but not an ester in the same molecule. Regioselectivity is preference for one position or orientation (e.g., Markovnikov addition), and stereoselectivity is preference for one stereoisomer.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'A/an _____ is an electron-rich species, with lone pairs or π bonds, that attacks electron-poor atoms',choices:['leaving group','Lewis acid','nucleophile','electrophile'],ans:2,exp:'A **nucleophile** ("nucleus-loving") donates an electron pair to an electron-poor center, so it acts as a Lewis base (e.g., OH⁻, CN⁻, NH₃, alkenes). An electrophile is the opposite: an electron-poor Lewis acid that accepts the pair.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'_____ is a kinetic measure of how quickly an electron-rich species attacks an electrophilic atom',choices:['Electrophilicity','Nucleophilicity','Acidity','Basicity'],ans:1,exp:'**Nucleophilicity** is kinetic (reaction rate), whereas basicity is thermodynamic (the equilibrium position of proton binding). They usually trend together, but in polar protic solvents larger, more polarizable anions (I⁻ > Br⁻ > Cl⁻ > F⁻) are better nucleophiles even though they are weaker bases.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'_____ are electron-poor species, often carrying a partial or full positive charge, that accept electron pairs',choices:['Nucleophiles','Leaving groups','Lewis bases','Electrophiles'],ans:3,exp:'**Electrophiles** ("electron-loving") accept an electron pair from a nucleophile, so they act as Lewis acids; examples are carbocations, carbonyl carbons and H⁺. Nucleophiles and Lewis bases are the electron-pair donors.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'_____ are molecular fragments that keep the bonding electrons after heterolysis',choices:['Nucleophiles','Leaving groups','Electrophiles','Carbocations'],ans:1,exp:'**Leaving groups** depart with the bonding electron pair during heterolytic bond cleavage. The best leaving groups are weak bases that can stabilize the extra negative charge (I⁻, Br⁻, Cl⁻, tosylate, H₂O); the carbon left behind may become a carbocation.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'Cl⁻ and Br⁻ are _____ leaving groups',choices:['good','poor','very poor','moderate'],ans:0,exp:'Cl⁻ and Br⁻ are **good** leaving groups because they are weak bases (conjugate bases of the strong acids HCl and HBr) whose charge is stabilized by their large size. Rule of thumb: the weaker the base, the better the leaving group (I⁻ > Br⁻ > Cl⁻ >> F⁻).'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'–OH is a _____ leaving group',choices:['good','poor','excellent','moderate'],ans:1,exp:'Hydroxide is a strong base (the conjugate base of the weak acid water), so –OH is a **poor** leaving group. Protonating it to –OH₂⁺ (leaving as H₂O) or converting it to a tosylate or mesylate turns it into a good leaving group.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'The "SN" in SN1 and SN2 reactions refers to _____',choices:['radical substitution','nucleophilic substitution','electrophilic substitution','nucleophilic addition'],ans:1,exp:'**SN** stands for substitution, nucleophilic: a nucleophile replaces a leaving group on an sp³ carbon. The number gives the molecularity of the rate-determining step (1 = unimolecular, 2 = bimolecular); nucleophilic addition is instead typical of aldehydes and ketones, which have no leaving group.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'The "1" in SN1 indicates that the rate-determining step of the reaction is _____',choices:['unimolecular','termolecular','a single concerted step','bimolecular'],ans:0,exp:'The 1 means the rate-determining step is **unimolecular**: only the substrate is involved as it ionizes to a carbocation, so rate = k[substrate]. SN1 actually has two steps (ionization, then nucleophilic attack); it is SN2 that is a single concerted, bimolecular step.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'An SN1 reaction has _____ step(s)',choices:['one','two','three','four'],ans:1,exp:'SN1 has **two** steps: (1) the leaving group departs to form a planar carbocation (slow, rate-determining) and (2) the nucleophile attacks the carbocation (fast). A proton-transfer step may follow when the nucleophile is neutral (e.g., water), but the core mechanism is two steps.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'An SN2 reaction has _____ step(s)',choices:['two','one','three','four'],ans:1,exp:'SN2 is **one** concerted step: the nucleophile attacks from the back side as the leaving group leaves, through a single pentacoordinate transition state with no intermediate. That single bimolecular step is why rate = k[substrate][nucleophile].'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'Substitution at a chiral carbon by the _____ mechanism gives a (nearly) racemic mixture of products',choices:['SN1','E1','E2','SN2'],ans:0,exp:'In **SN1** the leaving group departs first, leaving a planar sp² carbocation that the nucleophile can attack from either face, giving roughly equal amounts of both configurations. SN2 instead gives inversion, and E1/E2 form alkenes rather than substitution products.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'_____ reactions lead to inversion of stereochemistry at the reacting carbon',choices:['SN2','E2','SN1','E1'],ans:0,exp:'In **SN2** the nucleophile must attack from the side opposite the leaving group (backside attack), flipping the carbon\'s configuration like an umbrella in the wind (Walden inversion). SN1 scrambles stereochemistry through a planar carbocation.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'The rate law for an SN1 reaction is _____',choices:['rate = k[substrate]','rate = k[substrate][nucleophile]','rate = k[nucleophile]','rate = k[substrate]²'],ans:0,exp:'SN1\'s rate-determining step is unimolecular ionization of the substrate to a carbocation, so **rate = k[substrate]** (first order) and the nucleophile\'s concentration doesn\'t matter. Adding more nucleophile speeds up SN2, not SN1.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'The rate law for an SN2 reaction is _____',choices:['rate = k[substrate]','rate = k[substrate][nucleophile]','rate = k[nucleophile]','rate = k[substrate]²[nucleophile]'],ans:1,exp:'SN2 is a single bimolecular step in which the nucleophile and substrate collide, so **rate = k[substrate][nucleophile]**, first order in each and second order overall. Doubling either concentration doubles the rate.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'A primary substrate with a good nucleophile will usually react by _____',choices:['E1','E2','SN2','SN1'],ans:2,exp:'Primary carbons are unhindered for backside attack but would form very unstable primary carbocations, so they react by **SN2** (and never SN1/E1). Only a strong, bulky base such as tert-butoxide pushes a primary substrate toward E2.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'A tertiary substrate with a weak nucleophile in a polar protic solvent will undergo substitution by _____',choices:['SN1','E2','E1','SN2'],ans:0,exp:'Tertiary carbons are too hindered for backside SN2 attack but form stable carbocations, which polar protic solvents stabilize, so substitution goes by **SN1** (usually with competing E1). With a strong or bulky base, tertiary substrates undergo E2 instead.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'(CH₃)₃C–Br reacting with water at room temperature to give tert-butanol proceeds by which mechanism?',choices:['SN1','E1','SN2','E2'],ans:0,exp:'A tertiary alkyl halide with a weak nucleophile (water) in a polar protic solvent ionizes to a stable 3° carbocation that water then captures: **SN1**, with rate = k[(CH₃)₃CBr]. SN2 is blocked by steric hindrance, and since the product is an alcohol (substitution), the elimination mechanisms are ruled out.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'SN2 reactions prefer _____ substrates',choices:['secondary only','methyl and primary','tertiary only','secondary and tertiary'],ans:1,exp:'SN2 needs an unhindered back side, so reactivity runs **methyl > primary** > secondary, and tertiary substrates essentially never react by SN2. SN1 shows the opposite trend because it depends on carbocation stability (3° > 2°).'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'A methyl substrate cannot undergo elimination because elimination forms _____, which requires a second carbon next to the one bearing the leaving group',choices:['a ring','a C=C double bond','a C≡C triple bond','a new C–H bond'],ans:1,exp:'Elimination removes the leaving group and a β-hydrogen to form **a C=C double bond**, so a β-carbon is required. CH₃–X has only one carbon, so it can react only by SN2.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'Strong, bulky bases favor _____ reactions',choices:['SN2','SN1','E1','E2'],ans:3,exp:'Strong bases favor bimolecular elimination, **E2**: the base removes a β-H in the same concerted step that the leaving group departs (anti-periplanar geometry), so rate = k[substrate][base]. Bulky bases such as tert-butoxide can\'t reach the carbon for SN2 and give the less substituted (Hofmann) alkene.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'A secondary substrate with a weak nucleophile in a polar protic solvent tends to react by _____',choices:['SN1','SN2','E2','E1'],ans:0,exp:'Polar protic solvents stabilize the carbocation and leaving group and weaken nucleophiles by hydrogen bonding to them, so a secondary substrate with a weak nucleophile follows **SN1** (with competing E1). Secondary substrates are the "it depends" case, so solvent and nucleophile decide the mechanism.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'A secondary substrate with a strong, weakly basic nucleophile (e.g., CN⁻ or I⁻) in a polar aprotic solvent tends to react by _____',choices:['E1','SN1','SN2','E2'],ans:2,exp:'Polar aprotic solvents (DMSO, acetone, DMF) don\'t hydrogen bond to anions, leaving the nucleophile "naked" and reactive, so a secondary substrate goes **SN2**. If the nucleophile were also a strong base (e.g., an alkoxide), E2 would compete.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'_____ solvents have an O–H or N–H bond and can donate hydrogen bonds',choices:['Polar protic','Polar aprotic','Nonpolar','Aromatic'],ans:0,exp:'**Polar protic** solvents (water, alcohols, carboxylic acids, ammonia) have H bonded to O or N, so they hydrogen bond. They stabilize carbocations and leaving groups (favoring SN1/E1) but solvate and weaken anionic nucleophiles.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'_____ solvents are polar but have no O–H or N–H bond, so they cannot donate hydrogen bonds',choices:['Polar protic','Polar aprotic','Nonpolar','Aromatic'],ans:1,exp:'**Polar aprotic** solvents (DMSO, DMF, acetone, acetonitrile) dissolve ionic nucleophiles without hydrogen bonding to them, leaving the anions highly reactive. That is why they favor SN2.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'As a solvent, acetic acid is classified as _____',choices:['polar protic','polar aprotic','nonpolar','aromatic'],ans:0,exp:'Acetic acid (CH₃COOH) has an O–H group that can donate hydrogen bonds, so it is **polar protic**, like water and alcohols. Acetone and DMSO, by contrast, are polar aprotic.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'_____ use the suffix –ol or the prefix hydroxy-',choices:['Aldehydes','Ketones','Alcohols','Ethers'],ans:2,exp:'**Alcohols** contain –OH and take the suffix –ol (e.g., ethanol) when –OH is the highest-priority group, or the prefix hydroxy- when a higher-priority group such as a carbonyl or carboxylic acid is present. Aldehydes use –al and ketones –one.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'1°, 2° and 3° alcohols are classified according to the number of carbons attached to _____',choices:['the hydroxyl-bearing carbon','the oxygen atom','the terminal carbon','the most substituted carbon'],ans:0,exp:'The classification counts the carbons bonded to the **carbinol carbon** (the C bearing –OH): one = 1°, two = 2°, three = 3°. This matters for oxidation: 1° → aldehyde/acid, 2° → ketone, and 3° alcohols resist oxidation.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'A compound with a hydroxyl group bonded directly to a benzene ring is known as a/an _____',choices:['anisole','benzyl alcohol','quinone','phenol'],ans:3,exp:'A **phenol** has –OH directly on an aromatic ring. Phenols (pKa ≈ 10) are much more acidic than ordinary alcohols because the phenoxide ion is resonance-stabilized; in benzyl alcohol the –OH sits on a CH₂ attached to the ring, not on the ring itself.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'Two substituents on adjacent carbons (1,2-positions) of a benzene ring are in the _____ position',choices:['ortho','para','meta','ipso'],ans:0,exp:'**Ortho** = 1,2 (adjacent), meta = 1,3, para = 1,4 (opposite). Ipso refers to the ring carbon that already bears the substituent.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'_____ are conjugated cyclic diketones that are not aromatic',choices:['Phenols','Catechols','Hydroquinones','Quinones'],ans:3,exp:'**Quinones** (e.g., 1,4-benzoquinone) have two C=O groups in a six-membered ring; the ring is conjugated but lacks a continuous aromatic π system, so it is not aromatic. They form by oxidizing phenols or hydroquinones and are reduced back to hydroquinones, which are aromatic.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'_____ are produced by oxidation of quinones, which adds hydroxyl groups to the ring',choices:['Phenols','Hydroquinones','Hydroxyquinones','Ubiquinols'],ans:2,exp:'Oxidizing a quinone further adds –OH groups to the ring, giving **hydroxyquinones**. Hydroquinones are the opposite: they form when a quinone is reduced (both C=O become –OH), and ubiquinol is the reduced form of ubiquinone.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'_____ is a biologically active quinone that carries electrons from complexes I and II to complex III of the electron transport chain',choices:['Ubiquinone','FADH₂','Vitamin K','Cytochrome c'],ans:0,exp:'**Ubiquinone** (coenzyme Q) is a lipid-soluble mobile carrier in the inner mitochondrial membrane. It is reduced to ubiquinol (QH₂) by complex I (from NADH) and complex II (from succinate via FADH₂), and QH₂ passes the electrons to complex III; cytochrome c then carries them from complex III to complex IV.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'When an alcohol is oxidized it becomes one of the following compounds: _____, _____ or _____',choices:['aldehyde / ketone / carboxylic acid','alkane / alkene / alkyne','ether / ester / alkene','amine / amide / ester'],ans:0,exp:'Oxidizing a 1° alcohol gives an **aldehyde** (with mild PCC) or a **carboxylic acid** (with strong oxidants), and oxidizing a 2° alcohol gives a **ketone**. 3° alcohols resist oxidation; forming an alkene from an alcohol is dehydration, not oxidation.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'Treating a primary alcohol with pyridinium chlorochromate (PCC) forms a/an _____',choices:['ketone','carboxylic acid','aldehyde','ester'],ans:2,exp:'PCC is a mild, anhydrous oxidant that stops a 1° alcohol at the **aldehyde**; without water, the aldehyde can\'t form the hydrate that would be oxidized further. Strong oxidants (KMnO₄, chromic acid) go on to the carboxylic acid, and a 2° alcohol gives a ketone with either.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'Alcohols can be converted to _____ or _____ to make them better leaving groups for nucleophilic substitution reactions',choices:['acetals / ketals','mesylates / tosylates','esters / anhydrides','amides / imides'],ans:1,exp:'–OH is a poor leaving group because OH⁻ is a strong base. Converting it to a **mesylate or tosylate** (sulfonate ester) creates a resonance-stabilized, weakly basic leaving group that departs easily in SN reactions, without changing the carbon\'s configuration in the conversion step.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'Aldehydes and ketones can be protected by converting them into _____ or _____',choices:['hemiacetals / hemiketals','esters / amides','acetals / ketals','anhydrides / lactones'],ans:2,exp:'Reacting an aldehyde or ketone with two equivalents of alcohol (or a diol) under acid gives an **acetal or ketal**, which is stable to bases, nucleophiles and hydride reagents and is removed later with aqueous acid. Hemiacetals and hemiketals are unstable intermediates and can\'t serve as protecting groups.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'A/an _____ group contains a basic nitrogen atom with a localized lone pair of electrons',choices:['amide','amine','nitro','imide'],ans:1,exp:'In an **amine** (RNH₂, R₂NH, R₃N) the nitrogen\'s lone pair is free to accept a proton, so amines are weak bases (conjugate acid pKa ≈ 10–11). In amides and imides the lone pair is delocalized into the carbonyl, so they are essentially non-basic.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'An amide functional group has a/an _____ group linked to a/an _____ atom',choices:['carbonyl / nitrogen','hydroxyl / nitrogen','carbonyl / oxygen','amino / sulfur'],ans:0,exp:'An **amide** is a carbonyl carbon bonded to nitrogen (R–CO–NR₂). The nitrogen lone pair is delocalized into the C=O, so amides are planar, non-basic, and the least reactive carboxylic acid derivative; a carbonyl bonded to –OR would instead be an ester.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'_____ compounds contain carbon and hydrogen joined in straight chains, branched chains or non-aromatic rings',choices:['Aliphatic','Aromatic','Heterocyclic','Antiaromatic'],ans:0,exp:'**Aliphatic** compounds are the non-aromatic hydrocarbons and their derivatives: alkanes, alkenes, alkynes and cycloalkanes. Aromatic compounds such as benzene have a planar, cyclic, conjugated ring with 4n + 2 π electrons.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'A peptide bond forms when the _____ of one amino acid attacks the _____ of another amino acid',choices:['carbonyl carbon / amino group','amino group / carbonyl carbon','carboxylate oxygen / α-carbon','amino group / α-carbon'],ans:1,exp:'The nucleophilic **amino group** nitrogen (N-terminus) attacks the electrophilic **carbonyl carbon** of the other amino acid\'s carboxyl group (C-terminus), and water is lost (condensation/dehydration). In cells the carboxyl is first activated by attachment to tRNA, and the ribosome catalyzes the reaction.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'In a peptide bond, rotation about the C–N bond is restricted because of _____',choices:['resonance','hydrogen bonding','induction','steric hindrance'],ans:0,exp:'The nitrogen lone pair is delocalized into the carbonyl, giving the C–N bond partial double-bond character by **resonance**. That makes the peptide unit planar and rigid; rotation occurs only around the bonds to the α-carbon (φ and ψ angles).'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'Polypeptides are made up of multiple _____ linked by _____',choices:['fatty acids / ester bonds','amino acids / peptide bonds','monosaccharides / glycosidic bonds','nucleotides / phosphodiester bonds'],ans:1,exp:'Polypeptides are chains of **amino acids** joined by **peptide** (amide) bonds between one residue\'s carboxyl carbon and the next residue\'s amino nitrogen. The other pairs describe triacylglycerols, polysaccharides and nucleic acids.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'_____ and _____ are two common methods of synthesizing amino acids in the lab',choices:['Wittig reaction / Michael addition','Fischer esterification / Diels-Alder reaction','Strecker synthesis / Gabriel synthesis','Edman degradation / Sanger sequencing'],ans:2,exp:'The two MCAT-tested lab syntheses of amino acids are the **Strecker synthesis** (aldehyde + NH₄Cl + KCN, then hydrolysis of the aminonitrile) and the **Gabriel (malonic ester) synthesis** (potassium phthalimide + diethyl bromomalonate + alkyl halide). Both give racemic products; Edman degradation and Sanger methods sequence peptides rather than make amino acids.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'Strecker synthesis generates an amino acid from a/an _____',choices:['aldehyde','carboxylic acid','alcohol','ester'],ans:0,exp:'In the **Strecker synthesis** an aldehyde condenses with ammonia to form an imine, cyanide adds to give an α-aminonitrile, and acid hydrolysis converts the nitrile to the carboxylic acid. The aldehyde\'s R group becomes the amino acid side chain.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'_____ generates an amino acid from potassium phthalimide, diethyl bromomalonate and an alkyl halide',choices:['Strecker synthesis','Grignard reaction','Fischer esterification','Gabriel synthesis'],ans:3,exp:'In the **Gabriel synthesis** phthalimide supplies the nitrogen by SN2 on diethyl bromomalonate, the malonate α-carbon is alkylated with an alkyl halide (the future side chain), and hydrolysis plus decarboxylation releases the amino acid. The Strecker synthesis starts from an aldehyde, ammonia and cyanide instead.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'_____, in its deprotonated forms, is referred to as inorganic phosphate (Pi) or a phosphate group',choices:['Phosphoric acid','Carbonic acid','Nitric acid','Sulfuric acid'],ans:0,exp:'**Phosphoric acid** (H₃PO₄) loses protons to give H₂PO₄⁻, HPO₄²⁻ and PO₄³⁻; at physiological pH, inorganic phosphate (Pi) is mainly a mix of H₂PO₄⁻ and HPO₄²⁻. Phosphate groups are central to ATP, DNA and protein phosphorylation.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'Phosphoric acid is an excellent buffer because it contains three acidic hydrogens, each with _____',choices:['the same pKa','no measurable pKa','a different pKa','a pKa of 7'],ans:2,exp:'H₃PO₄ has three ionizable protons with **different pKa values** (about 2.1, 7.2 and 12.4), so it buffers near each of them. The pKa near 7.2 makes the H₂PO₄⁻/HPO₄²⁻ pair an important intracellular buffer.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'_____ make up the backbone of DNA',choices:['Phosphodiester bonds','Phosphoanhydride bonds','Glycosidic bonds','Phosphoramide bonds'],ans:0,exp:'**Phosphodiester bonds** link the 3′-OH of one sugar to the 5′-phosphate of the next, forming the sugar-phosphate backbone, which is negatively charged at physiological pH. Phosphoanhydride bonds are the high-energy links in ATP, and N-glycosidic bonds attach the bases to the sugars.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'_____ are carbon-containing compounds that also have phosphate groups',choices:['Organic phosphates','Organosulfates','Organonitrates','Organochlorides'],ans:0,exp:'**Organic phosphates** (phosphate esters) include ATP, nucleotides, phospholipids and sugar phosphates such as glucose-6-phosphate. Their phosphoanhydride and phosphoester bonds store energy and carry negative charge, which keeps metabolites trapped inside cells.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'An aldehyde is a terminal functional group containing a/an _____ bonded to at least one _____',choices:['carbonyl / hydrogen','hydroxyl / carbon','carbonyl / oxygen','amino / hydrogen'],ans:0,exp:'An **aldehyde** has a carbonyl carbon bonded to at least one hydrogen (R–CHO), so it always sits at the end of a chain. A carbonyl bonded to two carbons is a ketone, and one bonded to –OH is a carboxylic acid.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'_____ have the suffix -al',choices:['Esters','Ketones','Carboxylic acids','Aldehydes'],ans:3,exp:'**Aldehydes** use the suffix -al (e.g., ethanal = acetaldehyde) and the prefix oxo- when a higher-priority group is present. Ketones use -one, carboxylic acids -oic acid, and esters -oate.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'_____ use the suffix -one and the prefixes oxo- or keto-',choices:['Ketones','Carboxylic acids','Esters','Aldehydes'],ans:0,exp:'**Ketones** take -one (e.g., propanone = acetone), and oxo- or keto- as prefixes when a higher-priority group is present. Aldehydes, the other carbonyl family, use -al.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'In IUPAC naming, the aldehyde carbon is given the locant _____',choices:['1','2','3','4'],ans:0,exp:'The aldehyde carbon is always at the end of the chain, so it is **carbon 1** and its locant is usually omitted from the name (e.g., butanal, not 1-butanal). A ketone carbonyl is internal, so it needs a number (e.g., 2-butanone).'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'A ketone is an internal functional group containing a/an _____ bonded to two _____',choices:['carbonyl / alkyl (carbon) groups','hydroxyl / alkyl groups','carbonyl / hydrogens','amino / carbonyl groups'],ans:0,exp:'A **ketone** is a carbonyl whose carbon is bonded to two carbon (alkyl or aryl) groups, so it is internal, never at the end of a chain. One H on the carbonyl carbon would make it an aldehyde.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'A/an _____ group is a carbon-oxygen double bond (C=O)',choices:['alkoxy','carboxyl','carbonyl','hydroxyl'],ans:2,exp:'The **carbonyl** group (C=O) is polarized because O is more electronegative, making the carbon electrophilic; it is found in aldehydes, ketones, carboxylic acids and their derivatives. A carboxyl group (–COOH) contains a carbonyl plus an –OH.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'Aldehydes and ketones are synthesized from alcohols through _____',choices:['reduction','addition','hydrolysis','oxidation'],ans:3,exp:'**Oxidation** removes hydrogen from the carbinol carbon: 1° alcohols give aldehydes and 2° alcohols give ketones. The reverse, reduction with NaBH₄ or LiAlH₄, turns aldehydes and ketones back into alcohols.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'A mild oxidizing agent such as _____ must be used to make an aldehyde from a primary alcohol, or else oxidation continues to the carboxylic acid',choices:['LiAlH₄','KMnO₄','H₂CrO₄','PCC'],ans:3,exp:'**PCC** (pyridinium chlorochromate) works without water, so the aldehyde can\'t form the hydrate that strong oxidants would oxidize further. KMnO₄ and chromic acid (H₂CrO₄) go all the way to the carboxylic acid, and LiAlH₄ is a reducing agent.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'KMnO₄ and H₂CrO₄ (chromic acid, Jones reagent) are _____ oxidizing agents',choices:['strong','mild','weak','very weak'],ans:0,exp:'KMnO₄ and chromic acid (H₂CrO₄, made from Na₂Cr₂O₇ or CrO₃ in aqueous H₂SO₄) are **strong** oxidants: they take 1° alcohols all the way to carboxylic acids and 2° alcohols to ketones. PCC is the mild oxidant that stops 1° alcohols at the aldehyde.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'NaBH₄ is a _____ reducing agent',choices:['mild','strong','very strong','nonselective'],ans:0,exp:'NaBH₄ is a **mild** hydride donor: it reduces aldehydes and ketones to alcohols but generally not carboxylic acids, esters or amides, which makes it chemoselective. Those less reactive carbonyls need the strong reductant LiAlH₄.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'A strong reducing agent such as _____ is needed to reduce a carboxylic acid or an ester to an alcohol',choices:['LiAlH₄','NaBH₄','PCC','KMnO₄'],ans:0,exp:'Only a strong hydride donor such as **LiAlH₄** (LAH) reduces carboxylic acids and esters to 1° alcohols. NaBH₄ is too mild, and PCC and KMnO₄ are oxidizing agents, not reducing agents.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'In nucleophilic addition, the nucleophile attacks the partially positive _____ of the carbonyl group',choices:['hydrogen atom','nitrogen atom','carbon atom','oxygen atom'],ans:2,exp:'Oxygen pulls electron density out of the C=O bond, leaving the carbonyl **carbon** partially positive (electrophilic). The nucleophile adds to that carbon, pushing the π electrons onto oxygen to form a tetrahedral alkoxide intermediate.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'In a hydration reaction, water adds to a carbonyl to form a/an _____',choices:['geminal diol','hemiacetal','acetal','ketal'],ans:0,exp:'Water adds across C=O to give a **geminal diol** (hydrate), with both –OH groups on the same carbon; the equilibrium usually favors the carbonyl except for formaldehyde and other electron-poor aldehydes. Adding an alcohol instead of water gives a hemiacetal.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'When one equivalent of alcohol reacts with an aldehyde, a/an _____ is formed',choices:['hemiacetal','acetal','geminal diol','hemiketal'],ans:0,exp:'One equivalent of alcohol adds to an aldehyde to give a **hemiacetal** (one –OH and one –OR on the same carbon); a second equivalent under acid gives the full acetal. Ketones give hemiketals instead, and cyclic hemiacetals form the ring structures of sugars such as glucose.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'When one equivalent of alcohol reacts with a ketone, a/an _____ is formed',choices:['ketal','enol ether','hemiketal','geminal diol'],ans:2,exp:'Nucleophilic addition of one alcohol to a ketone carbonyl gives a **hemiketal**: one –OH and one –OR on the former carbonyl carbon. A hemiacetal is the same product from an aldehyde, and a second equivalent of alcohol (acid-catalyzed) converts a hemiketal to a ketal.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'When two equivalents of alcohol react with an aldehyde, a/an _____ is formed',choices:['ketal','hemiacetal','geminal diol','acetal'],ans:3,exp:'An aldehyde plus one alcohol gives a hemiacetal; under acid, loss of water and attack by a second alcohol give an **acetal** (two –OR groups on one carbon). The ketone-derived version is a ketal, and acetals are used as carbonyl protecting groups.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'When two equivalents of alcohol react with a ketone, a/an _____ is formed',choices:['hemiacetal','geminal diol','ketal','acetal'],ans:2,exp:'A ketone plus two alcohols (acid catalysis, loss of water) gives a **ketal**, with two –OR groups on the former carbonyl carbon. Acetals come from aldehydes; a geminal diol forms when water, not alcohol, adds to the carbonyl.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'Ammonia and primary amines react with aldehydes and ketones, with loss of water, to form _____',choices:['imines','amides','enamines','cyanohydrins'],ans:0,exp:'The amine nitrogen adds to the carbonyl carbon and water is eliminated, giving an **imine** (C=N), a condensation reaction. Secondary amines cannot form a neutral C=N and give enamines instead; amides come from carboxylic acid derivatives, not aldehydes or ketones.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'Imines can tautomerize to form _____',choices:['enols','amines','oximes','enamines'],ans:3,exp:'Imine–enamine tautomerization moves a proton from the α-carbon to nitrogen and shifts the double bond to C=C, giving an **enamine**. It is the nitrogen analog of keto–enol tautomerism; enols are the oxygen version.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'Hydrogen cyanide (HCN) reacts with aldehydes and ketones to form _____',choices:['enamines','hemiacetals','imines','cyanohydrins'],ans:3,exp:'Cyanide ion attacks the carbonyl carbon and the alkoxide is protonated, giving a **cyanohydrin**: an –OH and a –C≡N on the same carbon. Hydrolysis of the nitrile can then give an α-hydroxy acid.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'A/an _____ is formed when an α-hydrogen of an aldehyde or ketone is removed by a base',choices:['enamine','enol','imine','enolate'],ans:3,exp:'α-Hydrogens are acidic (pKa ≈ 20) because the resulting **enolate** anion is resonance-stabilized, with negative charge shared between the α-carbon and oxygen. An enol is the neutral tautomer, formed by moving a proton rather than removing it.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'Aldehydes are usually _____ reactive toward nucleophilic addition than ketones because of both steric and electronic effects',choices:['less','much less','no more','more'],ans:3,exp:'Aldehydes are **more** reactive: with only one alkyl group there is less steric hindrance to attack, and less electron donation to the carbonyl carbon, so it is more electrophilic. Note that aldehydes and ketones undergo nucleophilic addition, not substitution, because they lack a leaving group.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'The term enol comes from the fact that it is a/an _____ that is also a/an _____',choices:['alkene / alcohol','alkane / alcohol','alkyne / alcohol','alkene / aldehyde'],ans:0,exp:'An en-ol is an **alkene** (-ene) bearing an –OH (**alcohol**, -ol) on one of the double-bonded carbons. It is the tautomer of an aldehyde or ketone, not an aldehyde itself.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'_____ refers to the equilibrium between a carbonyl compound and an isomer bearing an –OH on a carbon of a C=C',choices:['Keto-enol tautomerization','Aldol condensation','Resonance','Racemization'],ans:0,exp:'**Keto-enol tautomerization** interconverts constitutional isomers by moving a proton and a double bond, catalyzed by acid or base. It is not resonance: resonance structures differ only in electron placement, whereas tautomers differ in where an atom (H) is attached.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'At equilibrium, a simple ketone such as acetone exists predominantly in the _____ form',choices:['keto','enol','enolate','hydrate'],ans:0,exp:'The **keto** form dominates because the C=O bond is stronger than the C=C bond it trades for in the enol. Enol content rises only with special stabilization, such as conjugation and intramolecular hydrogen bonding in 1,3-dicarbonyls.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'In a Michael addition, a/an _____ attacks the _____, forming a new C–C bond',choices:['enolate / β-carbon of an α,β-unsaturated carbonyl','enolate / carbonyl carbon of an aldehyde','enol / α-carbon of an α,β-unsaturated carbonyl','carbocation / β-carbon of an α,β-unsaturated carbonyl'],ans:0,exp:'A Michael (1,4-conjugate) addition has an **enolate** nucleophile attack the electrophilic **β-carbon of an α,β-unsaturated carbonyl**, whose partial positive charge comes from resonance with C=O. Enolate attack on an aldehyde carbonyl carbon is the aldol reaction instead.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'Removal of a proton from the less substituted (and less hindered) α-carbon gives the _____ enolate',choices:['kinetic','thermodynamic','more stable','more substituted'],ans:0,exp:'The less hindered α-proton is removed fastest, giving the **kinetic** enolate, favored by a bulky strong base (LDA), low temperature (−78 °C) and short reaction times. The thermodynamic enolate is the more substituted, more stable one.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'Removal of a proton from the more substituted (and more hindered) α-carbon gives the _____ enolate',choices:['kinetic','thermodynamic','less stable','less substituted'],ans:1,exp:'The **thermodynamic** enolate has the more substituted C=C, which makes it more stable, and it is favored by higher temperatures, longer times and smaller or weaker bases that allow equilibration. The kinetic enolate forms faster at the less hindered carbon.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'The name aldol reflects that the product is both a/an _____ and a/an _____ (in that order)',choices:['aldehyde / alcohol','alkene / alcohol','aldehyde / alkene','alkane / alcohol'],ans:0,exp:'Aldol = **ald**ehyde + alcoh**ol**: the aldol addition product is a β-hydroxy aldehyde (or β-hydroxy ketone). Dehydrating it later produces the alkene of an α,β-unsaturated carbonyl.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'Aldol addition is a powerful way to create a _____ bond',choices:['carbon-carbon','carbon-oxygen','carbon-nitrogen','carbon-hydrogen'],ans:0,exp:'In aldol addition the enolate α-carbon attacks another carbonyl carbon, forming a new **carbon-carbon** bond and building a larger carbon skeleton. The C–O bond of the product\'s –OH comes from the original carbonyl oxygen, not a new bond.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'An aldol condensation starts with a/an _____ and, after dehydration, produces a/an _____',choices:['aldol addition / α,β-unsaturated carbonyl','aldol addition / β-hydroxy carbonyl','Michael addition / α,β-unsaturated carbonyl','Claisen condensation / β-keto ester'],ans:0,exp:'Aldol condensation = **aldol addition** (giving a β-hydroxy carbonyl) followed by dehydration (loss of H₂O) to give an **α,β-unsaturated carbonyl**. The β-hydroxy carbonyl is the intermediate before dehydration, not the final product.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'In a base-catalyzed aldol reaction, the nucleophile is the _____ formed by deprotonating the α-carbon',choices:['enolate','enol','carbocation','alkoxide'],ans:0,exp:'Base removes an acidic α-hydrogen to give a resonance-stabilized **enolate**, whose α-carbon attacks the electrophilic carbonyl. The neutral enol is the nucleophile only under acid catalysis, and an alkoxide forms later when the carbonyl oxygen is attacked.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'The aldol electrophile is a/an _____ or _____',choices:['aldehyde / ketone','ester / carboxylic acid','alcohol / ether','alkene / alkyne'],ans:0,exp:'The enolate attacks the carbonyl carbon of an **aldehyde** or **ketone**; aldehydes are the better electrophiles because they are less hindered. Carboxylic acids are deprotonated by the base and esters undergo Claisen condensation instead.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'Base-catalyzed cleavage of a β-hydroxy carbonyl into two smaller carbonyl compounds is a/an _____',choices:['retro-aldol reaction','aldol condensation','Claisen condensation','decarboxylation'],ans:0,exp:'A **retro-aldol reaction** breaks the C–C bond between the α- and β-carbons, the reverse of aldol addition. In glycolysis, aldolase uses this reaction to cleave fructose-1,6-bisphosphate into DHAP and glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'_____ functional groups contain a carbonyl and a hydroxyl group connected to the same carbon',choices:['Amide','Ester','Carboxylic acid','Anhydride'],ans:2,exp:'A **carboxylic acid** (–COOH) has C=O and –OH on one carbon; resonance stabilization of the carboxylate makes it acidic (pKa ≈ 4–5). Esters replace the –OH with –OR and amides with –NR₂.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'Carboxylic acids use the suffix _____',choices:['–ol','–al','–oic acid','–one'],ans:2,exp:'Carboxylic acids take the suffix **–oic acid** (e.g., ethanoic acid), and the carboxyl carbon is C-1. –ol marks alcohols, –al aldehydes and –one ketones.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'Carboxylic acids have a high boiling point due to _____',choices:['London dispersion forces','dipole-dipole forces','ion-dipole forces','hydrogen bonding'],ans:3,exp:'Carboxylic acids form strong **hydrogen bonds** and pair up as hydrogen-bonded dimers, so they boil higher than alcohols of similar mass. Dipole-dipole forces are present too, but they are much weaker.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'The acidity of a carboxylic acid can be enhanced by substituents that are electron-_____',choices:['withdrawing','donating','releasing','rich'],ans:0,exp:'**Electron-withdrawing** groups (e.g., halogens, especially near the carboxyl) pull electron density by induction and stabilize the carboxylate anion, lowering pKa. Trichloroacetic acid (pKa ≈ 0.7) is much stronger than acetic acid (pKa ≈ 4.8).'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'The acidity of a carboxylic acid can be decreased by substituents that are electron-_____',choices:['withdrawing','donating','deficient','accepting'],ans:1,exp:'**Electron-donating** groups (e.g., alkyl groups) push electron density toward the carboxylate, destabilizing its negative charge and raising pKa. Withdrawing (accepting) groups have the opposite effect.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'Carboxylic acids can be made by the _____ of 1° alcohols or aldehydes',choices:['addition','hydrolysis','oxidation','reduction'],ans:2,exp:'Strong oxidants such as KMnO₄ or chromic acid **oxidize** a 1° alcohol through the aldehyde to the carboxylic acid. Reduction goes the other way, turning acids into alcohols with LiAlH₄.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'In nucleophilic acyl substitution a _____ displaces the _____',choices:['nucleophile / leaving group','leaving group / nucleophile','electrophile / leaving group','nucleophile / electrophile'],ans:0,exp:'The **nucleophile** adds to the carbonyl carbon to form a tetrahedral intermediate, which collapses to re-form C=O and expel the **leaving group**. Reactivity follows leaving-group ability: acid chloride > anhydride > ester > amide.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'CH₃CONH₂ is named by adding which suffix to the parent ethan-?',choices:['–amide','–amine','–oate','–oic acid'],ans:0,exp:'Amides replace the –oic acid ending with **–amide**, so CH₃CONH₂ is ethanamide (acetamide). –amine is for amines, which lack the carbonyl, and –oate is for esters.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'Amides are derivatives of carboxylic acids in which the _____ group has been replaced by a/an _____',choices:['hydroxyl / amino group','hydroxyl / alkoxy group','carbonyl / amino group','hydroxyl / acyloxy group'],ans:0,exp:'Amides form when the **hydroxyl** of a carboxylic acid is replaced by an **amino group** (–NH₂, –NHR or –NR₂). Replacing it with an alkoxy group (–OR) gives an ester; with an acyloxy group, an anhydride.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'A/an _____ is a carboxylic acid derivative in which –OH is replaced with –OR',choices:['amide','anhydride','acid chloride','ester'],ans:3,exp:'An **ester** (RCOOR′) has an alkoxy group in place of the acid\'s –OH and is made by Fischer esterification (acid + alcohol, acid catalyst). Esters give fruits their smells and form triacylglycerols.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'Esters are given the suffix _____',choices:['–amide','–ol','–oic acid','–oate'],ans:3,exp:'Esters are named alkyl + **–oate**: the alcohol-derived group comes first, then the acid name with –oic acid changed to –oate (e.g., ethyl ethanoate). –oic acid is the parent acid\'s suffix.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'A carboxylic acid can act as a nucleophile and attack a second carboxylic acid to form a/an _____',choices:['amide','anhydride','ester','acid chloride'],ans:1,exp:'Condensing two carboxylic acids with loss of water gives an **anhydride** (RCO–O–COR). Anhydrides are the second most reactive acid derivative, after acid chlorides.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'Linear and cyclic compounds formed by condensing two carboxylic acids are named by replacing the word acid with _____',choices:['anhydride','oate','oyl chloride','amide'],ans:0,exp:'Anhydrides keep the acid name and swap acid for **anhydride**: ethanoic anhydride (acetic anhydride), or succinic anhydride for the cyclic one. –oate names esters, –oyl chloride acid chlorides, and –amide amides.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'A carboxylic acid can be reduced to a 1° alcohol with a strong reducing agent like _____',choices:['LiAlH₄','NaBH₄','PCC','KMnO₄'],ans:0,exp:'**LiAlH₄** is a strong hydride donor that reduces carboxylic acids (and esters) all the way to 1° alcohols. NaBH₄ is too mild to reduce acids, and PCC and KMnO₄ are oxidants.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'_____ is a chemical reaction that removes a carboxyl group and releases carbon dioxide (CO₂)',choices:['Decarboxylation','Dehalogenation','Deoxygenation','Dehydration'],ans:0,exp:'**Decarboxylation** loses CO₂; β-keto acids and malonic acids decarboxylate easily on heating through a cyclic six-membered transition state. Biochemically, pyruvate dehydrogenase decarboxylates pyruvate to acetyl-CoA. Dehydration removes water, not CO₂.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'_____ is the hydrolysis of the ester bonds of triacylglycerols using a strong base such as NaOH or KOH',choices:['Transesterification','Saponification','Fischer esterification','Hydrogenation'],ans:1,exp:'**Saponification** is base-promoted ester hydrolysis that turns fats into glycerol and fatty acid salts (soap). It is irreversible because the carboxylate product is deprotonated; Fischer esterification is the acid-catalyzed formation of esters.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'A/an _____ dissolves nonpolar organic molecules in its interior, and can be solvated with water due to its exterior shell of hydrophilic groups',choices:['vesicle','liposome','emulsion','micelle'],ans:3,exp:'A **micelle** is a sphere of amphipathic molecules (soaps, bile salts) with nonpolar tails inward and polar heads outward, so it carries grease or dietary lipids through water. Vesicles and liposomes are bilayers with an aqueous interior.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'Carboxylic acids are converted to acid chlorides with _____ or _____',choices:['SOCl₂ / PCl₅','NaBH₄ / LiAlH₄','PCC / CrO₃','H₂O / H₂SO₄'],ans:0,exp:'Thionyl chloride (**SOCl₂**) and phosphorus halides such as **PCl₅** (or PCl₃) replace the –OH with –Cl, giving the most reactive carboxylic acid derivative. NaBH₄ and LiAlH₄ are reductants, and PCC/CrO₃ are oxidants.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'_____ describes when a reaction cannot proceed (or significantly slows) because substituents crowd the reactive site',choices:['Ring strain','Steric hindrance','Resonance','Induction'],ans:1,exp:'**Steric hindrance** is physical crowding that blocks the approach of a reagent; it is why SN2 fails at tertiary carbons and why ketones are less reactive than aldehydes. Induction and resonance are electronic effects, not spatial ones.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'_____ refers to uneven distribution of charge across a σ bond because of differences in electronegativity',choices:['Steric hindrance','Resonance','Induction','Ring strain'],ans:2,exp:'**Induction** transmits electron withdrawal or donation through σ bonds and fades quickly with distance. Resonance delocalizes electrons through π systems instead.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'Increased ring strain generally makes a molecule _____',choices:['more reactive','less reactive','more stable','unreactive'],ans:0,exp:'Angle and torsional strain raise a ring\'s energy, so ring-opening reactions release that strain and proceed readily, making strained rings **more reactive**. This is why β-lactams (penicillins) and epoxides are reactive.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'_____ is the process of exchanging the R″ group of an ester with the R′ group of an alcohol',choices:['Hydrolysis','Transesterification','Fischer esterification','Saponification'],ans:1,exp:'In **transesterification**, an alcohol attacks the ester carbonyl and swaps its alkoxy group for the original one, under acid or base catalysis. It is how biodiesel is made from triglycerides. Hydrolysis uses water and gives the carboxylic acid instead.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'When a phosphodiester bond is formed, a/an _____ is released',choices:['ATP','orthophosphate (Pi)','AMP','pyrophosphate (PPi)'],ans:3,exp:'The 3′-OH of the growing nucleic acid strand attacks the α-phosphate of an incoming nucleoside triphosphate, releasing **pyrophosphate (PPi)**. Hydrolysis of PPi to two Pi by pyrophosphatase makes polymerization essentially irreversible.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'A high Ka (low pKa) indicates a _____ acid',choices:['strong','weak','dilute','concentrated'],ans:0,exp:'Ka measures how far HA dissociates, so a large Ka means a **strong** acid. Strength is not concentration: a dilute strong acid still dissociates completely, and a concentrated weak acid still has a small Ka.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'_____ is the pH at which half of the species is deprotonated',choices:['pKa','pKb','pI','pKw'],ans:0,exp:'From Henderson–Hasselbalch, when [A⁻] = [HA], log(1) = 0, so pH = **pKa**; this is the half-equivalence point of a titration. The pI is different: it is the pH at which an amino acid or protein has zero net charge.'},
  {s:'Reactions & Mechanisms',q:'The number 2 in an SN2 reaction refers to the fact that the reaction is _____',choices:['termolecular','bimolecular','unimolecular','two-step'],ans:1,exp:'SN2 means substitution, nucleophilic, **bimolecular**: both the substrate and the nucleophile take part in the single concerted rate-determining step, so rate = k[substrate][Nu]. SN2 is one step, not two.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'Cyclic amides are called _____',choices:['lactides','lactols','lactones','lactams'],ans:3,exp:'Cyclic amides are **lactams** (e.g., the β-lactam ring of penicillin); the "m" matches amide. Cyclic esters are lactones, and lactols are cyclic hemiacetals.'},
  {s:'Carbonyl Chemistry',q:'Cyclic esters are called _____',choices:['lactones','lactams','lactides','lactols'],ans:0,exp:'Cyclic esters are **lactones**, formed by intramolecular esterification of hydroxy acids (5- and 6-membered rings form most easily). Lactams are the cyclic amides.'},
  {s:'Bonding & Structure',q:'Geminal diols have their two hydroxyl groups attached to _____ carbon(s); vicinal diols have theirs attached to _____ carbon(s)',choices:['the same / adjacent','adjacent / the same','adjacent / adjacent','the same / the same'],ans:0,exp:'Geminal (gem = twin) diols have both –OH groups on **the same** carbon, as in carbonyl hydrates. Vicinal (vicinus = neighbor) diols have them on **adjacent** carbons, as in ethylene glycol.'},
  {s:'Spectroscopy',q:'_____ measures absorption of light that causes molecular vibrations such as stretching, bending, twisting and folding',choices:['Mass spectrometry','IR spectroscopy','NMR spectroscopy','UV-Vis spectroscopy'],ans:1,exp:'**IR spectroscopy** detects bond vibrations, which identify functional groups (e.g., O–H, C=O). A vibration is IR-active only if it changes the dipole moment. UV-Vis instead detects electronic transitions.'},
  {s:'Spectroscopy',q:'A compound with formula C₄H₁₁N (no oxygen) shows two medium peaks near 3300–3400 cm⁻¹ in its IR spectrum. These peaks come from _____ stretching',choices:['N–H','O–H','C≡N','C=C'],ans:0,exp:'**N–H** stretches appear near 3300 cm⁻¹; a primary amine (–NH₂) shows two peaks (symmetric and asymmetric stretches), and a secondary amine shows one. O–H is impossible without oxygen, C≡N absorbs near 2200 cm⁻¹, and C=C near 1650 cm⁻¹.'},
  {s:'Spectroscopy',q:'An IR spectrum with a very broad absorption at 2500–3300 cm⁻¹ and a strong, sharp peak near 1710 cm⁻¹ indicates which two bonds?',choices:['O–H / C=O','N–H / C=O','C–H / C=C','O–H / C≡C'],ans:0,exp:'A very broad **O–H** band overlapping the C–H region plus a strong **C=O** near 1710 cm⁻¹ is the signature of a carboxylic acid. An amide N–H band is narrower, and its C=O sits lower, near 1650–1690 cm⁻¹.'},
  {s:'Spectroscopy',q:'_____ measures absorption of light that promotes electrons between molecular orbitals (e.g., π → π*), and is most useful for conjugated systems',choices:['IR spectroscopy','UV-Vis spectroscopy','NMR spectroscopy','Mass spectrometry'],ans:1,exp:'**UV-Vis spectroscopy** detects electronic transitions from the HOMO to the LUMO. More conjugation narrows the HOMO–LUMO gap and shifts absorption to longer wavelengths. IR detects bond vibrations, not electron promotion.'},
  {s:'Spectroscopy',q:'_____ measures absorption of radio-frequency energy by nuclei whose spins align with or against an applied magnetic field',choices:['UV-Vis spectroscopy','NMR spectroscopy','IR spectroscopy','Mass spectrometry'],ans:1,exp:'**NMR spectroscopy** detects nuclei with spin (¹H, ¹³C) flipping between spin states in a magnetic field. The resonance frequency depends on each nucleus\'s electronic environment, revealing connectivity. MRI is the clinical application.'},
  {s:'Spectroscopy',q:'NMR spectra are calibrated using _____, which has a chemical shift of 0 ppm',choices:['tetramethylsilane','chloroform','deuterium oxide','benzene'],ans:0,exp:'**Tetramethylsilane (TMS)** gives one sharp, highly shielded signal (12 equivalent H) and is defined as 0 ppm. Residual CHCl₃ in CDCl₃ appears at 7.26 ppm, so chloroform is not the zero reference.'},
  {s:'Spectroscopy',q:'The _____ is the area under each peak (signal) on the NMR spectrum. It corresponds to the number of protons in that signal',choices:['coupling constant','chemical shift','multiplicity','integration'],ans:3,exp:'**Integration** gives the relative number of protons producing each signal (e.g., 3:2 for an ethyl group). Multiplicity reports neighboring protons (n+1 rule), and chemical shift reports electronic environment.'},
  {s:'Spectroscopy',q:'_____ in NMR spectroscopy is when a nucleus\' chemical shift is increased due to the removal of electron density, magnetic induction, or other effects',choices:['Integration','Coupling','Shielding','Deshielding'],ans:3,exp:'**Deshielding**: electron-withdrawing groups pull electron density away from a nucleus, so it feels more of the applied field and resonates at higher ppm (downfield). Shielding by surrounding electrons does the opposite.'},
  {s:'Spectroscopy',q:'In NMR spectroscopy, the _____ is the position on the δ scale (in ppm) where the peak occurs',choices:['chemical shift','integration','coupling constant','multiplicity'],ans:0,exp:'The **chemical shift** (δ, ppm) reflects a nucleus\'s electronic environment relative to TMS (0 ppm). The coupling constant (J, in Hz) is the spacing between peaks within a split signal.'},
  {s:'Spectroscopy',q:'In NMR spectroscopy, a peak that is deshielded appears _____',choices:['downfield (left)','upfield (right)','at lower ppm','as a singlet'],ans:0,exp:'Deshielded protons appear **downfield**, to the left at higher ppm (e.g., aldehyde H at 9–10 ppm). Shielding has no direct effect on splitting, so it says nothing about whether a peak is a singlet.'},
  {s:'Spectroscopy',q:'In NMR spectroscopy, a peak that is shielded appears _____',choices:['downfield (left)','upfield (right)','at higher ppm','as a singlet'],ans:1,exp:'Electron density shields a nucleus from the applied field, so shielded protons appear **upfield**, to the right at lower ppm, near TMS (e.g., alkyl H at 0.7–1.9 ppm).'},
  {s:'Spectroscopy',q:'When hydrogens are on adjacent atoms, they interfere with each other\'s magnetic environment, causing _____, also known as _____',choices:['spin-spin coupling / splitting','integration / peak area','chemical shift / deshielding','shielding / upfield shift'],ans:0,exp:'Neighboring nonequivalent protons perturb each other\'s local field, splitting each signal into n + 1 peaks; this is **spin-spin coupling**, also called **splitting**. Integration (peak area) counts protons instead.'},
  {s:'Spectroscopy',q:'In NMR spectroscopy, the multiplicity of a proton or group of protons is given by the n+1 rule, where n = _____',choices:['the number of protons on adjacent carbons','the number of protons in the signal itself','the number of carbons in the molecule','the number of equivalent protons on the same carbon'],ans:0,exp:'n is the number of nonequivalent **protons on adjacent carbons**: a CH₃ next to a CH₂ appears as a triplet (2 + 1). The protons within the signal itself determine its integration, not its splitting.'},
  {s:'Spectroscopy',q:'The hydrogens of a/an _____ group have an NMR range of 0.7–1.9 ppm',choices:['vinylic','alkyl','aryl','aldehyde'],ans:1,exp:'sp³ **alkyl** C–H protons are well shielded and appear far upfield at 0.7–1.9 ppm. Vinylic (4.5–6.5), aryl (6.5–8.0) and aldehyde (9–10) protons are increasingly deshielded.'},
  {s:'Spectroscopy',q:'A/an _____ hydrogen has an NMR range of 2.5–3.0 ppm',choices:['alkynyl','vinylic','aryl','methyl ketone'],ans:0,exp:'Terminal **alkynyl** H (≡C–H) appears at about 2.5–3.0 ppm, surprisingly upfield of vinylic H because the triple bond\'s cylindrical π cloud shields it along the bond axis. Methyl ketone H appear a little lower, at 2.0–2.4 ppm.'},
  {s:'Spectroscopy',q:'The hydrogens of a/an _____ have an NMR range of 2.0–2.4 ppm',choices:['alkynyl','vinylic','methyl ketone','aldehyde'],ans:2,exp:'The CH₃ of a **methyl ketone** sits next to the electron-withdrawing C=O, which deshields it to 2.0–2.4 ppm. The aldehyde H is bonded directly to the carbonyl carbon and appears much farther downfield (9–10 ppm).'},
  {s:'Spectroscopy',q:'_____ hydrogens have an NMR range of 4.5–6.5 ppm',choices:['Alkyl','Aryl','Aldehyde','Vinylic'],ans:3,exp:'**Vinylic** H (on a C=C) are deshielded by the π system to 4.5–6.5 ppm. Aryl H appear farther downfield (6.5–8.0 ppm) because the aromatic ring current deshields them further.'},
  {s:'Spectroscopy',q:'The hydrogens of a/an _____ group have an NMR range of 6.5–8.0 ppm',choices:['alkyl','aryl','vinylic','aldehyde'],ans:1,exp:'**Aryl** (aromatic) H appear at 6.5–8.0 ppm because the ring current of the delocalized π electrons strongly deshields protons on the ring\'s edge. Vinylic H appear at 4.5–6.5 ppm.'},
  {s:'Spectroscopy',q:'The hydrogens of a/an _____ group have an NMR range of 9.7–10.0 ppm',choices:['aldehyde','carboxylic acid','alcohol','ketone'],ans:0,exp:'The **aldehyde** H sits directly on the carbonyl carbon and is strongly deshielded (≈ 9–10 ppm). Ketones have no such H, and carboxylic acid O–H appears even farther downfield (10.5–12 ppm).'},
  {s:'Spectroscopy',q:'The hydrogens of a/an _____ group have an NMR range of approximately 11.0–12.0 ppm',choices:['aryl','carboxylic acid','vinylic','aldehyde'],ans:1,exp:'The **carboxylic acid** O–H is the most downfield common signal (≈ 10.5–12 ppm), deshielded by the carbonyl and by hydrogen bonding, and it is usually broad. Aldehyde H appear just below, at 9–10 ppm.'},
  {s:'Spectroscopy',q:'The largest peak in the mass spectrum, corresponding to the most abundant ion, is called the _____',choices:['M+2 peak','molecular ion peak','base peak','M+1 peak'],ans:2,exp:'The **base peak** is the tallest peak and is set to 100% relative abundance; it usually comes from the most stable fragment cation. The molecular ion peak gives the molecular weight and is often not the tallest.'},
  {s:'Spectroscopy',q:'Ignoring small isotope peaks, the ion with the greatest m/z in a mass spectrum is usually the _____',choices:['base peak','molecular ion','largest fragment ion','acylium ion'],ans:1,exp:'The **molecular ion** (M⁺) is the intact molecule minus one electron, so its m/z equals the molecular weight and it is the heaviest ion apart from M+1/M+2 isotope peaks. The base peak is the most abundant ion, which is often a fragment.'},
  {s:'Spectroscopy',q:'In a mass spectrum, the _____ is a small peak 1 m/z unit to the right of the main molecular ion peak',choices:['base peak','M+1 peak','molecular ion peak','M+2 peak'],ans:1,exp:'The **M+1 peak** comes mostly from molecules containing one ¹³C (1.1% natural abundance), so its height relative to M estimates the number of carbons. The M+2 peak, two units higher, flags Cl or Br.'},
  {s:'Spectroscopy',q:'In a mass spectrum, the M+2 peak shows the relative abundance of either _____ or _____',choices:['³⁷Cl / ⁸¹Br','¹³C / ²H','¹⁵N / ¹⁷O','³⁵Cl / ⁷⁹Br'],ans:0,exp:'Heavy halogen isotopes give the M+2 peak: **³⁷Cl** makes M:M+2 ≈ 3:1 and **⁸¹Br** makes M:M+2 ≈ 1:1. ³⁵Cl and ⁷⁹Br are the lighter isotopes that make up the M peak, and ¹³C produces the M+1 peak.'},
  {s:'Separations & Lab Techniques',q:'A/an _____ combines two immiscible liquids, one of which easily dissolves the compound of interest',choices:['chromatography','recrystallization','extraction','distillation'],ans:2,exp:'An **extraction** uses two immiscible layers (usually aqueous and organic) in a separatory funnel; the compound partitions into the layer it dissolves in best ("like dissolves like"). Acid–base extractions change a compound\'s charge to move it between layers.'},
  {s:'Separations & Lab Techniques',q:'In an extraction, the organic layer is the _____ layer',choices:['polar','aqueous','nonpolar','protic'],ans:2,exp:'The organic layer (e.g., ether, hexane) is the **nonpolar** layer and dissolves neutral, nonpolar compounds. The aqueous layer is the polar one and holds ions such as deprotonated acids and protonated amines.'},
  {s:'Separations & Lab Techniques',q:'In an extraction, the aqueous (water) layer is the _____ layer and dissolves ionic and hydrogen-bonding compounds',choices:['organic','aprotic','polar','nonpolar'],ans:2,exp:'Water is highly **polar** (and protic), so the aqueous layer dissolves ionic and hydrogen-bonding compounds such as carboxylate salts and ammonium salts. The organic layer is the nonpolar one.'},
  {s:'Separations & Lab Techniques',q:'A/an _____ is the reverse of an extraction: a small amount of solvent that dissolves impurities is run over the compound of interest',choices:['recrystallization','chromatography','filtration','wash'],ans:3,exp:'In an extraction the solvent pulls the desired compound out of a mixture; in a **wash** a small amount of solvent removes impurities and leaves the desired compound behind. An example is washing an organic layer with brine or bicarbonate.'},
  {s:'Separations & Lab Techniques',q:'During a filtration, a solid, also known as the _____, is isolated from a liquid, also known as the _____',choices:['residue / filtrate','filtrate / residue','solute / solvent','solvent / solute'],ans:0,exp:'The solid caught on the filter paper is the **residue**, and the liquid that passes through is the **filtrate**. Solute and solvent describe the components of a solution, not the products of filtration.'},
  {s:'Separations & Lab Techniques',q:'_____ filtration can be used to collect solid product, although _____ filtration is generally used for this purpose because it is faster',choices:['Gravity / vacuum','Vacuum / gravity','Gravity / centrifugal','Hot / gravity'],ans:0,exp:'**Gravity** filtration is slow and is mainly used when the product is in the filtrate (often hot filtration to remove insoluble impurities). **Vacuum** filtration (Büchner funnel) uses suction to pull the solvent through quickly and is the usual way to collect a solid.'},
  {s:'Separations & Lab Techniques',q:'_____, also known as _____, is a procedure for purifying an impure solid compound in a solvent',choices:['Recrystallization / fractional crystallization','Distillation / fractional distillation','Sublimation / deposition','Extraction / partitioning'],ans:0,exp:'**Recrystallization** (**fractional crystallization**) dissolves the impure solid in a minimum of hot solvent in which it is soluble hot but not cold; on cooling, pure crystals form while impurities stay in solution. Distillation purifies liquids, not solids.'},
  {s:'Separations & Lab Techniques',q:'Chromatography separates the components of a mixture based on _____',choices:['differing affinities for a stationary versus a mobile phase','differences in boiling point alone','differences in density between immiscible liquids','differences in solubility in hot versus cold solvent'],ans:0,exp:'Each component partitions between a **stationary phase** and a moving **mobile phase**; components with more affinity for the stationary phase travel more slowly. Boiling point alone describes distillation, and hot/cold solubility describes recrystallization.'},
  {s:'Separations & Lab Techniques',q:'In normal-phase chromatography, the stationary phase is typically _____ and this causes _____ molecules to elute more slowly',choices:['polar / polar','nonpolar / polar','polar / nonpolar','nonpolar / nonpolar'],ans:0,exp:'Normal-phase stationary phases (silica, alumina) are **polar**, so **polar** compounds adsorb more strongly and elute more slowly. In reverse-phase HPLC the stationary phase is nonpolar and the order flips.'},
  {s:'Separations & Lab Techniques',q:'In normal-phase chromatography, the mobile phase is typically _____ and this causes _____ molecules to elute faster',choices:['polar / polar','nonpolar / nonpolar','polar / nonpolar','nonpolar / polar'],ans:1,exp:'The mobile phase is a relatively **nonpolar** solvent (e.g., hexane/ethyl acetate), so **nonpolar** compounds dissolve in it, interact weakly with the polar stationary phase and elute faster.'},
  {s:'Separations & Lab Techniques',q:'In a typical (silica) thin-layer chromatography setup, the spot that moves farthest is the _____ substance and the spot that moves the shortest distance is the _____ substance',choices:['polar / nonpolar','nonpolar / polar','nonpolar / nonpolar','polar / polar'],ans:1,exp:'On polar silica, **nonpolar** compounds interact weakly with the plate and travel farthest (high Rf), while **polar** compounds stick and travel least (low Rf). Rf = distance traveled by the spot ÷ distance traveled by the solvent front.'},
  {s:'Separations & Lab Techniques',q:'In liquid chromatography, _____ is a common stationary phase while _____ or another nonpolar liquid is used as the mobile phase',choices:['silica / hexane','hexane / silica','silica / water','alumina / water'],ans:0,exp:'Normal-phase column chromatography uses polar **silica** (or alumina) as the stationary phase and a nonpolar solvent such as **hexane** as the mobile phase. Water is highly polar, so it does not fit a nonpolar mobile phase.'},
  {s:'Separations & Lab Techniques',q:'_____ relies on pumps to pass a pressurized liquid solvent containing the sample mixture through a column filled with a solid adsorbent material',choices:['Gel filtration chromatography','Gas chromatography','High-performance liquid chromatography','Ion exchange chromatography'],ans:2,exp:'**High-performance liquid chromatography (HPLC)** forces solvent through a tightly packed column under high pressure, giving fast, high-resolution separations; it can run in normal or reverse phase. Gas chromatography uses an inert carrier gas, not a pressurized liquid.'},
  {s:'Separations & Lab Techniques',q:'In gas chromatography, the liquid sample is vaporized before separation. Molecules are separated based on their _____ and _____',choices:['volatility / polarity','size / net charge','density / solubility','color / molecular weight'],ans:0,exp:'A vaporized sample is carried by an inert gas through a column; compounds separate by **volatility** (boiling point) and by how strongly they interact with the stationary phase (**polarity**). More volatile, less interactive compounds elute first. Size and charge are the bases of gel filtration and ion exchange.'},
  {s:'Separations & Lab Techniques',q:'Gel-filtration chromatography separates molecules based on their _____',choices:['charge','hydrophobicity','ligand affinity','size'],ans:3,exp:'Gel-filtration (size-exclusion) chromatography separates by **size** (hydrodynamic volume): small molecules enter the bead pores and take a longer path. Charge is the basis of ion-exchange chromatography and ligand binding of affinity chromatography.'},
  {s:'Separations & Lab Techniques',q:'In size-exclusion chromatography, large molecules will elute _____',choices:['first','last','simultaneously','randomly'],ans:0,exp:'Large molecules are excluded from the bead pores and flow around the beads, so they elute **first**; small molecules wander through the pores and elute last. The common mistake is assuming larger molecules are "stuck" longer.'},
  {s:'Separations & Lab Techniques',q:'Ion exchange chromatography separates proteins by their _____',choices:['polarity','net charge','ligand affinity','size'],ans:1,exp:'Charged beads bind proteins of opposite **net charge**, which depends on buffer pH relative to each protein\'s pI. Bound proteins are then eluted with increasing salt concentration.'},
  {s:'Separations & Lab Techniques',q:'In anion exchange chromatography, the beads are _____ charged and this causes _____ charged proteins to elute first',choices:['positively / positively','negatively / positively','negatively / negatively','positively / negatively'],ans:0,exp:'Anion-exchange beads are **positively** charged and bind (exchange) anions, so negatively charged proteins are retained while **positively** charged proteins are repelled and elute first. The name refers to the ions exchanged, not the bead charge.'},
  {s:'Separations & Lab Techniques',q:'In cation exchange chromatography, the beads are _____ charged and this causes _____ charged proteins to elute first',choices:['negatively / negatively','negatively / positively','positively / positively','positively / negatively'],ans:0,exp:'Cation-exchange beads are **negatively** charged and retain positively charged proteins (cations), so **negatively** charged proteins pass through and elute first.'},
  {s:'Separations & Lab Techniques',q:'Affinity chromatography separates proteins based on _____',choices:['their size','their net charge','their polarity','specific ligand binding'],ans:3,exp:'The column carries an immobilized ligand (e.g., an antibody, substrate analog or Ni²⁺ for His-tags) that **binds the target protein specifically**; everything else washes through, and the target is eluted with free ligand or a competitor.'},
  {s:'Separations & Lab Techniques',q:'In a typical thin-layer chromatography setup, the stationary phase is made up of _____ which is _____',choices:['alumina / nonpolar','silica gel / polar','cellulose / nonpolar','silica gel / nonpolar'],ans:1,exp:'Standard TLC plates are coated with **silica gel**, which is highly **polar** because of its surface Si–OH groups, so polar compounds stick and move less. Reverse-phase plates use a nonpolar coating instead.'},
  {s:'Separations & Lab Techniques',q:'A distillation separates liquids according to differences in their _____',choices:['molecular weights','polarities','boiling points','melting points'],ans:2,exp:'Distillation exploits differences in **boiling points** (vapor pressures): the more volatile component vaporizes first and is condensed and collected. Melting points matter for solids, not for separating liquids.'},
  {s:'Separations & Lab Techniques',q:'Simple distillation can be used if the boiling points are under 150 °C and are at least _____ apart',choices:['5 °C','10 °C','50 °C','25 °C'],ans:3,exp:'Simple distillation works when the liquids boil below 150 °C and differ by at least **25 °C**. Closer boiling points need fractional distillation, and boiling points above 150 °C call for vacuum distillation.'},
  {s:'Separations & Lab Techniques',q:'Vacuum distillation should be used if the boiling points are over 150 °C to prevent degradation of the product. The vacuum _____, which decreases the boiling point of the liquid',choices:['lowers the ambient pressure','increases the polarity','increases the temperature','decreases the solvent volume'],ans:0,exp:'A liquid boils when its vapor pressure equals the external pressure, so **lowering the ambient pressure** lets it boil at a lower temperature, avoiding thermal decomposition. Raising the temperature is exactly what vacuum distillation avoids.'},
  {s:'Separations & Lab Techniques',q:'Fractional distillation is a process by which components in a mixture are separated into different parts (called fractions) according to their different _____',choices:['polarities','melting points','molecular weights','boiling points'],ans:3,exp:'A fractionating column packed with glass beads provides many vaporization–condensation cycles, so liquids whose **boiling points** differ by less than 25 °C can still be separated.'},
  {s:'Spectroscopy',q:'N–H bonds in IR spectroscopy appear at approximately which wavenumber (cm⁻¹)?',choices:['2200','1750','3300','1650'],ans:2,exp:'N–H stretches absorb near **3300 cm⁻¹** (about 3300–3500). Primary amines show two peaks and secondary amines one, and N–H bands are sharper than the broad O–H band in the same region. 1750 is C=O, 2200 is a triple bond, and 1650 is C=C.'},
  {s:'Spectroscopy',q:'O–H bonds in IR spectroscopy appear in which wavenumber range (cm⁻¹)?',choices:['1600–1680','1900–2200','3000–3300','1700–1750'],ans:2,exp:'Alcohol O–H appears as a broad band in the **3000–3300 cm⁻¹** region (often cited up to ~3600), broadened by hydrogen bonding. Carboxylic acid O–H is even broader (2500–3300). 1700–1750 is the C=O region.'},
  {s:'Spectroscopy',q:'C=O bonds (carbonyl groups) in IR spectroscopy appear at approximately which wavenumber (cm⁻¹)?',choices:['3300','2200','1600','1750'],ans:3,exp:'The carbonyl stretch is a strong, sharp peak near **1750 cm⁻¹** (about 1700–1750; ketones ~1715, acid chlorides ~1800, amides ~1680). It is usually the most intense peak in the spectrum, while 1600 belongs to C=C.'},
  {s:'Spectroscopy',q:'C=C double bonds in IR spectroscopy appear in which wavenumber range (cm⁻¹)?',choices:['3000–3300','1900–2200','1600–1680','1700–1750'],ans:2,exp:'C=C stretches absorb at **1600–1680 cm⁻¹**, weaker than C=O. Aromatic rings show peaks near 1600 and 1475. The 1700–1750 range belongs to C=O, the most common confusion.'},
  {s:'Spectroscopy',q:'C≡C and C≡N triple bonds in IR spectroscopy appear in which wavenumber range (cm⁻¹)?',choices:['3000–3300','1900–2200','1600–1680','1700–1750'],ans:1,exp:'Triple bonds absorb in the otherwise empty region Kaplan rounds to **1900–2200 cm⁻¹** (true ranges: C≡C ~2100–2260, often weak; C≡N ~2210–2260, sharp). Stronger bonds vibrate at higher frequency than double bonds (1600–1750).'},
  {s:'Spectroscopy',q:'In ¹H NMR spectroscopy, alkyl hydrogens (C–H) appear at what chemical shift range (ppm)?',choices:['4.5–6.5','9.7–10.0','0.7–1.9','2.0–2.4'],ans:2,exp:'sp³ alkyl protons are shielded and appear upfield at **0.7–1.9 ppm** (CH₃ ~0.9, CH₂ ~1.3). Nearby electron-withdrawing groups shift them downfield; e.g., a CH₃ next to C=O appears at 2.0–2.4 ppm.'},
  {s:'Spectroscopy',q:'In ¹H NMR spectroscopy, vinylic (alkene C=C–H) hydrogens appear at what chemical shift range (ppm)?',choices:['0.7–1.9','9.7–10.0','4.5–6.5','2.5–3.0'],ans:2,exp:'Vinylic protons appear at **4.5–6.5 ppm**, deshielded by the π electrons of the sp² carbon. Aromatic protons are even more deshielded (6.5–8.0) by the ring current, and 2.5–3.0 is terminal alkyne H.'},
  {s:'Spectroscopy',q:'In ¹H NMR spectroscopy, aldehyde hydrogens (CHO) appear at what chemical shift range (ppm)?',choices:['0.7–1.9','4.5–6.5','2.0–2.4','9.7–10.0'],ans:3,exp:'The aldehyde proton sits on the carbonyl carbon and is strongly deshielded, appearing at **9.7–10.0 ppm**. This distinguishes aldehydes from ketones, which lack this proton; 2.0–2.4 ppm is the α-H next to a carbonyl.'},
  {s:'Spectroscopy',q:'In ¹H NMR spectroscopy, carboxylic acid O–H protons appear at what chemical shift range (ppm)?',choices:['0.7–1.9','4.5–6.5','6.5–8.0','11.0–12.0'],ans:3,exp:'Carboxylic acid O–H protons are the most deshielded common signal, at about **11.0–12.0 ppm** (often 10.5–12), because of the electron-withdrawing carbonyl and hydrogen-bonded dimers; the peak is usually broad. 6.5–8.0 ppm is aromatic H.'},
  {s:'Spectroscopy',q:'What does the n+1 rule describe in ¹H NMR spectroscopy?',choices:['The number of chiral centers in the molecule','The number of signals expected for the molecule','The multiplicity of a signal, where n is the number of adjacent protons','The number of carbons, estimated from the M+1 peak'],ans:2,exp:'A signal with n nonequivalent neighboring protons splits into **n + 1 peaks**: one neighbor gives a doublet, two a triplet, three a quartet. Equivalent protons do not split each other, and the number of signals reflects the number of unique H environments instead.'},
  {s:'Spectroscopy',q:'In mass spectrometry, the M+1 peak is used to determine which property of the molecule?',choices:['Number of nitrogen atoms','Number of carbon atoms','Number of oxygen atoms','Number of halogen atoms'],ans:1,exp:'The M+1 peak comes mainly from ¹³C (1.1% natural abundance), so the **number of carbons** ≈ (M+1 height ÷ M height) ÷ 1.1%; e.g., M+1 = 4.4% of M suggests 4 carbons. Halogens (Cl, Br) show up at M+2 instead.'},
  {s:'Spectroscopy',q:'A compound shows M and M+2 peaks of equal intensity (1:1 ratio) in its mass spectrum. Which element does this indicate?',choices:['Chlorine','Bromine','Nitrogen','Sulfur'],ans:1,exp:'**Bromine** has two isotopes of nearly equal abundance, ⁷⁹Br (50.7%) and ⁸¹Br (49.3%), giving a ~1:1 M:M+2 pattern. Chlorine (³⁵Cl/³⁷Cl) gives ~3:1, and sulfur gives only a small M+2 (~4%).'},
  {s:'Spectroscopy',q:'In mass spectrometry, what does the molecular ion (M⁺) peak represent?',choices:['The most abundant fragment ion','The intact molecule minus one electron','A fragment formed by α-cleavage','The base peak of the spectrum'],ans:1,exp:'Electron bombardment knocks one electron off the molecule, giving the radical cation **M⁺**, whose m/z equals the molecular weight. The base peak is the most abundant ion, which is often a fragment rather than M⁺.'},
];
